// Handbook, Part 2: Interview probability. See handbook-content.js for how chapters are structured.
// Checks without an `id` keep their original order, so saved progress still matches them.
(function () {
  const Fig = QT.hbFig, N = Fig.normal();
  const choose = (n, k) => { let r = 1; for (let i = 1; i <= k; i++) r = (r * (n - k + i)) / i; return r; };
  const binom = (n, p) => Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, (_, k) => choose(n, k) * p ** k * (1 - p) ** (n - k));
  const fact = (n) => (n < 2 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1));
  const PATH = [0, 1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 0, 1, 2]; // for the reflection principle

  QT.hbChapters = Object.assign(QT.hbChapters || {}, {
    distributions: {
      title: 'The distributions you need', minutes: 50,
      why: 'Most random things in interviews and in markets are one of five shapes. Recognise the shape from the story, and you get the mean, the variance and a formula for free.',
      goals: ['Recognise the binomial, geometric, Poisson, exponential and normal from a description', 'Know each one\'s mean and variance without looking them up', 'Use the normal\'s 68–95–99.7 rule and its key quantiles for quick probabilities', 'Approximate a binomial by a normal, with the continuity correction', 'Race exponential clocks against each other', 'See how the five connect to each other'],
      prereq: ['expectation', 'variance'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Binomial: counting successes', blocks: [
          `<p>n independent trials, each a success with probability p. The number of successes X is <b>binomial</b>:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">P(X = k) = C(n, k) p<sup>k</sup>(1 − p)<sup>n − k</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; mean np &nbsp;&nbsp; variance np(1 − p)</div>
           <p>Each part has a job: p<sup>k</sup>(1 − p)<sup>n − k</sup> is the chance of one particular sequence, and C(n, k) counts the sequences. It's a sum of n independent 0/1 variables, which is where the mean and variance come from.</p>`,
          { fig: 'bars', vals: binom(10, 0.5), labs: [...Array(11).keys()], tone: (i) => (i === 3 ? 'bad' : ''), xl: 'heads in 10 flips', alt: 'Binomial distribution of heads in 10 flips, peaked at 5.', cap: 'Heads in 10 fair flips. Each bar is C(10, k)/1024.' },
          { widget: 'dist' },
          { q: 'A fair coin is flipped 10 times. What is the probability of exactly 3 heads?', a: 120 / 1024, tol: { abs: 0.001 }, hint: 'C(10, 3) ways, each with probability (½)¹⁰.', sol: 'Each particular sequence of 10 flips has probability (½)¹⁰ = 1/1024. The sequences with exactly 3 heads: choose which 3 flips are heads, C(10, 3) = 120. So 120/1024 ≈ <b>0.117</b>.' },
          { q: 'What is the standard deviation of the number of heads in 400 fair flips?', a: 10, hint: '√(np(1 − p)).', sol: 'Variance np(1 − p) = 400 × ½ × ½ = 100, so the standard deviation is √100 = <b>10</b> heads.' },
          `<p><b>The normal approximation.</b> When np(1 − p) is more than about 10, the binomial is close to a normal with the same mean and variance. Because the binomial only takes whole numbers, treat each value k as the interval from k − ½ to k + ½: the <b>continuity correction</b>. It matters most for moderate n.</p>`,
          { worked: 'at least 60 heads in 100', q: 'A fair coin is flipped 100 times. Roughly what is the probability of at least 60 heads?', steps: [
            ['Mean and standard deviation?', 'np = 50 and √(100 × ¼) = 5.'],
            ['"At least 60" in continuous terms, with the correction?', 'X ≥ 59.5, so z = (59.5 − 50)/5 = 1.9.'],
            'P(Z ≥ 1.9) ≈ <b>0.029</b>. The exact binomial answer is 0.0284; without the correction (z = 2) you\'d say 0.023, noticeably off.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Geometric: waiting for the first success', blocks: [
          `<p>Repeat independent trials until the first success. The number of trials is <b>geometric</b>: P(first success on trial k) = (1 − p)<sup>k − 1</sup>p, with mean 1/p and variance (1 − p)/p². "On average you wait 1/p tries" answers a surprising number of interview questions.</p>`,
          { proof: 'Let E be the expected wait. The first trial succeeds with probability p, or fails and leaves you where you started: E = 1 + (1 − p)E, so E = 1/p. This is first-step analysis, which has its own chapter soon.' },
          `<p>Two more facts. The chance you need <b>more than k</b> trials is the chance the first k all fail: (1 − p)<sup>k</sup>, which is usually easier than adding up the distribution. And the geometric is <b>memoryless</b>: after k failures, the number of extra trials you need has the same distribution as at the start. A die doesn't know it's "due" a six.</p>
           <p>Watch the convention: some books count the failures <i>before</i> the first success (mean (1 − p)/p) rather than the trials up to and including it (mean 1/p). Say which you mean.</p>`,
          { q: 'You roll a die until it shows a 1 or a 2. What is the expected number of rolls?', a: 3, hint: 'Each roll succeeds with probability 2/6.', sol: 'Each roll succeeds with probability p = 2/6 = 1/3, independently, so the number of rolls is geometric with mean 1/p = <b>3</b>.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Poisson: counting rare events', blocks: [
          `<p>When events happen independently at a steady average rate λ per unit of time (trades, arrivals, typos), the count in one unit is <b>Poisson</b>: P(X = k) = e<sup>−λ</sup> λ<sup>k</sup>/k!. Mean and variance are both λ.</p>
           <p>It's the limit of a binomial with many trials and a small p, with λ = np. So "1,000 lots, each with a 0.2% chance of failing" is Poisson with λ = 2, and sums of independent Poissons are Poisson with the rates added.</p>`,
          { fig: 'bars', vals: [...Array(11).keys()].map((k) => Math.exp(-3) * 3 ** k / fact(k)), labs: [...Array(11).keys()], xl: 'events in a period, λ = 3', alt: 'Poisson distribution with rate 3, peaked at 2 and 3.', cap: 'Poisson with λ = 3. The spread grows with the rate: the standard deviation is √λ.' },
          `<p>Where the formula comes from: split the minute into n tiny slots, each holding an event with probability λ/n. The count is binomial(n, λ/n). As n grows, P(k events) = C(n, k)(λ/n)<sup>k</sup>(1 − λ/n)<sup>n−k</sup> → λ<sup>k</sup>e<sup>−λ</sup>/k!, using (1 − λ/n)ⁿ → e<sup>−λ</sup>. So the Poisson is what "many independent tiny chances" looks like, and mean = variance = λ is inherited from np ≈ np(1 − p).</p>
           <p>A quick diagnostic for real data: if the variance of the counts is much bigger than the mean, events are clustering, and a Poisson model will understate how often busy periods happen.</p>`,
          { q: 'Trades arrive at an average of 2 a minute, independently. What is the probability of no trades in a given minute?', a: Math.exp(-2), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'P(X = 0) = e<sup>−λ</sup>.', sol: 'Poisson with λ = 2: P(0) = e<sup>−2</sup> 2⁰/0! = e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ <b>0.135</b>. So about one minute in seven is empty, even at 2 trades a minute.' },
          { id: 'x1', q: 'Same flow, 2 trades a minute on average. What is the probability of exactly 2 trades in a minute?', a: 2 * Math.exp(-2), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'e<sup>−λ</sup>λ<sup>k</sup>/k! with λ = 2, k = 2.', sol: 'e<sup>−2</sup> × 2²/2! = 2e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ <b>0.271</b>. The most likely counts are 1 and 2, tied at 0.271 each.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Exponential: waiting times with no memory', blocks: [
          `<p>The time between Poisson events is <b>exponential</b> with rate λ: P(T &gt; t) = e<sup>−λt</sup>, mean 1/λ. It's <b>memoryless</b>: P(T &gt; s + t | T &gt; s) = P(T &gt; t). Having already waited doesn't change how much longer you'll wait. That breaks most people's intuition.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 30], y: [0, 1], xl: 'minutes waited', yl: 'P(still waiting)', xt: [0, 10, 20, 30], yt: [0, 0.5, 1], fns: [{ f: (t) => Math.exp(-t / 10) }, { f: (t) => Math.exp(-(t - 10) / 10), from: 10, tone: 'bad', dash: true }], labels: [{ x: 15, y: 0.7, t: 'restart at 10: the same curve', tone: 'bad' }], alt: 'An exponential survival curve, and the same curve restarted at 10 minutes.', cap: 'Restart the clock at any point and the curve from there is the same shape. Only the exponential has this property.' },
          { q: 'Buses arrive as a Poisson process, one every 10 minutes on average. You have already waited 5 minutes. How many more minutes do you expect to wait?', a: 10, hint: 'Memorylessness.', sol: 'The wait is exponential with mean 10, and exponential waits are memoryless: P(T > 5 + t | T > 5) = e<sup>−(5+t)/10</sup>/e<sup>−5/10</sup> = e<sup>−t/10</sup>. So the remaining wait is still exponential with mean <b>10</b> minutes.' },
          `<p><b>Racing exponential clocks.</b> Suppose buy orders arrive at rate λ₁ and sell orders at rate λ₂, independently. Two facts follow from P(T > t) = e<sup>−λt</sup>:</p>
           <ul><li>The time until the <b>first</b> of them is exponential with rate λ₁ + λ₂, since P(both later than t) = e<sup>−λ₁t</sup>e<sup>−λ₂t</sup>.</li>
           <li>The first one is a buy with probability <b>λ₁/(λ₁ + λ₂)</b>, independent of when it happens.</li></ul>
           <p>These make many "which happens first" problems one line. Merging independent Poisson flows gives a Poisson flow with the rates added, and each event is a buy with probability λ₁/(λ₁ + λ₂).</p>`,
          { id: 'x2', q: 'Buy orders arrive at rate 2 a minute and sell orders at rate 3 a minute, as independent Poisson processes. What is the probability the next order is a buy?', a: 0.4, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'λ₁/(λ₁ + λ₂).', sol: '2/(2 + 3) = <b>0.4</b>. And the wait until the next order of either kind is exponential with rate 5: 12 seconds on average.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'Buses run exactly every 10 minutes, and you turn up at a random time. How long do you expect to wait, and how does that compare with Poisson buses at the same rate?', opts: [['5 minutes, half the Poisson wait'], ['10 minutes, the same as Poisson', 'With a regular timetable, you land somewhere in a 10-minute gap.'], ['5 minutes, the same as Poisson', 'A Poisson wait from a random moment averages 10 minutes.'], ['10 minutes, twice the Poisson wait', 'Try the uniform gap: what is its mean?']], sol: 'A random arrival in a fixed 10-minute gap waits 5 on average. Poisson gaps vary, and you\'re more likely to land in a long one, so you wait longer: the inspection paradox.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'The normal distribution', blocks: [
          `<p>Sums of many small independent effects are approximately <b>normal</b>. For a normal with mean μ and standard deviation σ: about 68% lies within 1σ, 95% within 2σ, and 99.7% within 3σ. To use it, <b>standardise</b>: z = (x − μ)/σ.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [-3.6, 3.6], y: [0, 0.44], xt: [[-2, '−2σ'], [-1, '−1σ'], [0, 'μ'], [1, '+1σ'], [2, '+2σ']], fns: [{ f: N, tone: 'ink' }], area: [{ f: N, from: -2, to: 2, tone: 'acc-soft' }, { f: N, from: -1, to: 1, tone: 'ok-soft' }], labels: [{ x: 0, y: 0.18, t: '68%', a: 'middle' }, { x: 1.5, y: 0.03, t: '95% in all', a: 'middle' }, { x: 2.75, y: 0.06, t: '2.5%', a: 'middle' }, { x: -2.75, y: 0.06, t: '2.5%', a: 'middle' }], alt: 'The bell curve with the central 68 and 95 percent shaded.', cap: 'The rule to carry in your head: 68–95–99.7, with 2.5% in each tail beyond 2σ.' },
          { q: 'X is normal with mean 100 and standard deviation 15. Roughly what is P(X > 130)?', a: 1 - 0.97725, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: '130 is 2 standard deviations above the mean; 95% lies within ±2σ.', sol: 'Standardise: z = (130 − 100)/15 = 2. About 5% lies outside ±2σ, half of it above: ≈ <b>0.025</b> (exactly 0.0228).' },
          `<p>Know these quantiles of the standard normal by heart; they come up in every confidence interval, test and risk number:</p>
           <table class="hb-table"><tr><th>z</th><th>P(Z &gt; z)</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr>
           <tr><td>1</td><td>16%</td><td>a one-sigma move</td></tr>
           <tr><td>1.645</td><td>5%</td><td>95% one-sided; 95% value at risk</td></tr>
           <tr><td>1.96</td><td>2.5%</td><td>95% two-sided confidence intervals</td></tr>
           <tr><td>2.326</td><td>1%</td><td>99% value at risk</td></tr>
           <tr><td>3</td><td>0.13%</td><td>"three sigma"</td></tr></table>
           <p>Two other properties do a lot of work. Sums of independent normals are normal, with the means and the variances added. And any normal is a shifted, scaled standard normal: X = μ + σZ, so every normal question reduces to a z-score.</p>`,
          { id: 'x3', q: 'Daily P&L is normal with mean 0 and standard deviation $1m. What loss is exceeded on only 1 day in 100 (the 99% value at risk), in $m?', a: 2.326, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'P(Z > 2.326) = 1%.', sol: 'The 1% tail starts 2.326 standard deviations below the mean: VaR ≈ <b>$2.33m</b>. (At 95% it would be 1.645 × $1m.) Fat tails in real P&L make the true figure larger.' },
          { market: 'Real returns have fatter tails than the normal. A 5σ day should come about once in 7,000 years if returns were normal, yet markets deliver them every few years. Use the normal for quick reasoning, and never to size risk on its own.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'How they connect', blocks: [
          `<table class="hb-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>The story</th><th>Mean</th><th>Variance</th></tr>
           <tr><td>Bernoulli</td><td>one yes/no trial</td><td>p</td><td>p(1 − p)</td></tr>
           <tr><td>Binomial</td><td>successes in n trials</td><td>np</td><td>np(1 − p)</td></tr>
           <tr><td>Geometric</td><td>trials to first success</td><td>1/p</td><td>(1 − p)/p²</td></tr>
           <tr><td>Poisson</td><td>events at rate λ</td><td>λ</td><td>λ</td></tr>
           <tr><td>Exponential</td><td>time between events</td><td>1/λ</td><td>1/λ²</td></tr>
           <tr><td>Uniform [a, b]</td><td>equally likely over a range</td><td>(a + b)/2</td><td>(b − a)²/12</td></tr>
           <tr><td>Normal</td><td>sum of many small effects</td><td>μ</td><td>σ²</td></tr></table>`,
          { fig: 'flow', alt: 'How distributions connect.', steps: ['Bernoulli', 'sum of n: Binomial', 'n large, p small: Poisson', 'gaps between events: Exponential'], cap: 'And the other way: a binomial with n large and p not small is close to a normal.' },
          { worked: 'spot the distribution', q: 'A desk gets on average 0.5 large orders an hour, independently. What is the chance of at least 2 in a 4-hour session?', steps: [
            ['What is the story?', 'Independent events at a steady rate, so Poisson.'],
            ['What is λ for 4 hours?', '0.5 × 4 = 2.'],
            ['"At least 2" is awkward. What is the complement?', '0 or 1 events: e<sup>−2</sup>(1 + 2) = 3e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ 0.406.'],
            'So the answer is 1 − 0.406 ≈ 0.594.',
          ] },
          { id: 'm2', q: 'You count how many of 50 stocks rose today. Which distribution should you be careful about using, and why?', opts: [['Binomial: trials are dependent'], ['Binomial, as p is too small', 'p near ½ is fine for a binomial.'], ['Binomial, as n is too large', 'Size isn\'t the issue.'], ['No problem at all: it\'s a binomial count', 'The market moves them together.']], sol: 'The binomial needs independent trials. Stocks share a market factor, so the count is far more spread out than a binomial predicts. Always check the assumptions, not just the shape.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Binomial: successes in n trials; mean np, variance np(1 − p). Normal approximation with the ± ½ correction.', 'Geometric: trials until the first success; mean 1/p; P(more than k) = (1 − p)<sup>k</sup>.', 'Poisson: rare events at rate λ; mean = variance = λ.', 'Exponential: memoryless waiting; mean 1/λ. The first of several clocks has the rates added, and clock i wins with probability λ<sub>i</sub>/Σλ.', 'Normal: 68–95–99.7; standardise with z = (x − μ)/σ; 1.645, 1.96, 2.326.', 'Check independence before trusting a formula.'],
      explain: { q: 'What does it mean for a waiting time to be memoryless? Give an example that is, and one that isn\'t.', a: 'The remaining wait has the same distribution however long you\'ve already waited. The time until the next trade in a Poisson flow is memoryless. The time until the next scheduled train isn\'t: the longer you\'ve waited, the sooner it comes.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'You roll a die 5 times. What is the probability of exactly 2 sixes?', a: 10 * 125 / 7776, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'C(5, 2)(1/6)²(5/6)³.', sol: '<p><b>Story:</b> 5 independent trials, success = a six (p = 1/6): binomial.</p><ol><li>Which rolls are the sixes: C(5, 2) = 10 choices.</li><li>Each such sequence: (1/6)² × (5/6)³ = 125/7776.</li><li>10 × 125/7776 = 1250/7776 ≈ <b>0.161</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> the mean number of sixes is 5/6 ≈ 0.83, so 0 or 1 six should be most likely (0.402 each) and 2 sixes well behind. 0.161 fits.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'X is Poisson with λ = 3. What is P(X ≤ 1)?', a: 4 * Math.exp(-3), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'P(0) + P(1).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> add the two probabilities, e<sup>−λ</sup>λ<sup>k</sup>/k!.</p><ol><li>P(0) = e<sup>−3</sup>.</li><li>P(1) = 3e<sup>−3</sup>.</li><li>Sum: 4e<sup>−3</sup> ≈ 4 × 0.0498 = <b>0.199</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Sense check:</b> with a mean of 3, seeing at most 1 should be fairly unlikely: about 1 in 5.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Each attempt succeeds with probability 0.2. What is the probability that you need more than 3 attempts?', a: 0.512, hint: 'That means the first 3 all fail.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> "more than 3 attempts" means the first 3 all failed, so no sum is needed.</p><ol><li>Each fails with probability 0.8, independently.</li><li>0.8³ = <b>0.512</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Point:</b> P(geometric > k) = (1 − p)<sup>k</sup> is the quickest geometric fact. Even at 20% a try, you need more than 3 tries about half the time.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Waits are exponential with a mean of 10 minutes. What is P(wait > 20 minutes)?', a: Math.exp(-2), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'e<sup>−t/mean</sup>.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> mean 10 means rate λ = 1/10, and P(T > t) = e<sup>−λt</sup>.</p><ol><li>P(T > 20) = e<sup>−20/10</sup> = e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ <b>0.135</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check with memorylessness:</b> P(T > 10) = e<sup>−1</sup> ≈ 0.37, and surviving another 10 has the same chance, so P(T > 20) = 0.37² ≈ 0.135 ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'X is normal with mean 50 and sd 10. Roughly what is P(30 &lt; X &lt; 70)?', a: 0.9545, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'That\'s ±2σ.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> standardise the ends.</p><ol><li>z = (30 − 50)/10 = −2 and z = (70 − 50)/10 = +2.</li><li>About 95% of a normal lies within ±2σ: <b>0.95</b> (exactly 0.9545).</li></ol><p>For exactly 95%, the interval is ±1.96σ, from 30.4 to 69.6.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: '500 flights each lose a bag with probability 1%, independently. Roughly what is the chance that no bags are lost?', a: Math.exp(-5), tol: { abs: 0.0005 }, hint: 'Poisson with λ = 5.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> many trials, small p: binomial ≈ Poisson with λ = np.</p><ol><li>λ = 500 × 0.01 = 5.</li><li>P(0) ≈ e<sup>−5</sup> ≈ <b>0.0067</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> exactly, 0.99⁵⁰⁰ = e<sup>500 ln 0.99</sup> ≈ e<sup>−5.025</sup> ≈ 0.0066. The approximation is excellent when p is small.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'Buy orders arrive as Poisson with rate 2 a minute, and sell orders as Poisson with rate 3 a minute, independently. What is the variance of the total orders in a minute?', a: 5, hint: 'Poissons add.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> independent Poisson counts add to a Poisson count with the rates added.</p><ol><li>Total ~ Poisson(2 + 3) = Poisson(5).</li><li>A Poisson\'s variance equals its mean: <b>5</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Without the Poisson fact:</b> independent variances add, 2 + 3 = 5. Same answer.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Prove that the exponential is memoryless, and show that the geometric is too.', sol: '<p><b>Exponential:</b> P(T > s + t | T > s) = P(T > s + t)/P(T > s) = e<sup>−λ(s+t)</sup>/e<sup>−λs</sup> = e<sup>−λt</sup> = P(T > t).</p><p><b>Geometric:</b> P(X > m + n | X > m) = (1 − p)<sup>m+n</sup>/(1 − p)<sup>m</sup> = (1 − p)<sup>n</sup> = P(X > n).</p><p><b>The deeper point:</b> these are the only memoryless distributions (continuous and discrete respectively). Memorylessness means "the process has no state other than whether the event has happened", which is exactly a constant hazard rate.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Sit near a road or a window for 10 minutes and write down the time of every passing car (or use arrivals on a live trade feed). Count arrivals per minute. Is the variance close to the mean, as a Poisson would predict?', sol: '<p>If the variance is much larger than the mean, arrivals are clustered: traffic lights release cars in groups, and in markets one trade triggers others. Real order flow is strongly clustered, so a Poisson model is only a starting point (self-exciting "Hawkes" processes are the usual next step).</p><p>A ratio of variance to mean near 1 suggests independent arrivals; below 1 suggests something regular, like a timetable.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Distributions topic', '#/topic/dist'], ['Formula cards', '#/flashcards']],
    },

    symmetry: {
      title: 'Symmetry and clever counting', minutes: 45,
      why: 'Interviewers love problems that are long if you grind and one line if you look. The look is nearly always symmetry: ask what can\'t matter, and let that do the work.',
      goals: ['Spot symmetries that make an answer obvious: relabelling, reflection and ordering', 'Use indicator variables on harder counting questions', 'Use the "equal gaps" argument for positions in a random order', 'Use "every ordering is equally likely" for records and ranks'],
      prereq: ['expectation', 'counting'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Symmetry first', blocks: [
          `<p>Before calculating, ask what can't matter. In a shuffled deck, the ace of spades is equally likely to be above or below the king of spades. Two players with identical chances have identical win probabilities. Symmetry often gives the answer outright, or reduces it to "½ of what's left".</p>
           <p>Three kinds of symmetry cover most puzzles:</p>
           <ul><li><b>Relabelling.</b> Swap two players, two cards, two colours. If the swap leaves the problem unchanged, their chances are equal.</li>
           <li><b>Reflection.</b> A fair die reads the same upside down: face k and face 7 − k are equally likely. So the number of heads H in n fair flips has the same distribution as n − H, and for two dice, max and 7 − min have the same distribution.</li>
           <li><b>Ordering.</b> Independent draws from the same continuous distribution are equally likely to come in any order. The chance the third of five is the largest is 1/5, whatever the distribution.</li></ul>
           <p>The skill is spotting what the problem doesn't care about. Write down two outcomes you think are equally likely and check that the rules really treat them the same.</p>`,
          { q: 'A deck is shuffled. What is the probability the ace of spades is above the king of spades?', a: 0.5, hint: 'Swap the two cards.', sol: 'Swapping the two cards turns every arrangement with the ace above into one with it below, and back again: a one-to-one pairing of equally likely shuffles. So the two orders are equally likely: <b>½</b>.' },
          { q: '4 fair coin flips. What is the probability of more heads than tails?', a: 5 / 16, hint: 'More heads, more tails, or a tie. The first two are symmetric.', sol: 'Three cases: more heads, more tails, or a tie. Swapping H and T shows the first two are equally likely. P(tie) = P(2 heads) = C(4, 2)/16 = 6/16. The rest splits evenly: (1 − 6/16)/2 = <b>5/16</b>. Check: 3 heads (4 ways) + 4 heads (1 way) = 5 of 16 ✓.' },
          { worked: 'reflection', q: 'The expected larger of two dice is 161/36. What is the expected smaller one?', steps: [
            ['How are the larger and smaller related?', 'Larger + smaller = the total of the two dice, whose mean is 7.'],
            'So E[min] = 7 − 161/36 = 91/36 ≈ <b>2.53</b>.',
            'Reflection says the same: replace each face k by 7 − k; the larger becomes 7 minus the smaller, and the dice are still fair.',
          ] },
          { worked: 'the clever one', q: 'You and I each flip fair coins: you flip 11, I flip 10. What is the chance you get more heads than me?', steps: [
            ['Set your last coin aside. Compare your first 10 with my 10. What are the three cases?', 'You\'re ahead, you\'re behind, or you\'re tied. By symmetry, ahead and behind have the same probability; call it a. The tie has probability t, with 2a + t = 1.'],
            ['Now add your last coin. When do you finish with more heads?', 'If you were ahead (you stay ahead), or if you were tied and the last coin is a head.'],
            'So P = a + t/2 = (2a + t)/2 = <b>½</b>, without computing anything.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Indicators for harder counts', blocks: [
          `<p>Linearity plus indicators handles counts that look hopeless. The number of runs in a sequence of flips is 1 plus the number of places where a flip differs from the one before. The number of empty boxes is a sum of "box i is empty" indicators.</p>`,
          { fig: 'strip', cell: 30, labelW: 80, rows: [{ label: 'flips', cells: ['H', 'H', 'T', 'H', 'T', 'T', 'H', 'H'] }, { label: 'switch?', cells: [{ gap: true }, 0, { t: 1, tone: 'acc' }, { t: 1, tone: 'acc' }, { t: 1, tone: 'acc' }, 0, { t: 1, tone: 'acc' }, 0] }], alt: 'Eight flips with a switch indicator at each position.', cap: '4 switches, so 5 runs: HH, T, H, TT, HH. Runs = switches + 1.' },
          { q: 'A coin is flipped 10 times. What is the expected number of runs (maximal blocks of the same side, e.g. HHTH has 3)?', a: 5.5, hint: 'Runs = 1 + the number of switches between consecutive flips.', sol: 'A run starts at the first flip and at every switch. There are 9 adjacent pairs, each a switch with probability ½: 1 + 9 × ½ = <b>5.5</b>.' },
          { q: '10 balls are dropped independently into 10 boxes. What is the expected number of empty boxes?', a: 10 * 0.9 ** 10, tol: { abs: 0.02 }, hint: 'Each box is empty with probability (9/10)¹⁰.', sol: 'Indicator per box. A box is missed by one ball with probability 9/10, so by all 10 with probability 0.9¹⁰ ≈ 0.349. Ten boxes: 10 × 0.349 ≈ <b>3.49</b>. In general n balls in n boxes leave about n/e empty, a third of them.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Equal gaps', blocks: [
          `<p>Place K special items at random among N in a row (say aces in a deck). By symmetry, the K items split the other N − K into K + 1 gaps of equal expected size: no gap is special, so they must all average the same.</p>`,
          { fig: 'strip', cell: 18, rows: [{ cells: ['', '', '', '', '', { t: 'A', tone: 'acc' }, '', '', '', '', '', '', '', { t: 'A', tone: 'acc' }, '', '', { t: 'A', tone: 'acc' }, '', '', '', '', { t: 'A', tone: 'acc' }, '', '', ''] }], wide: true, alt: 'A row of cards with four aces splitting it into five gaps.', cap: 'Four aces split 48 other cards into 5 gaps, each of average size 48/5 = 9.6.' },
          `<p>So the first special item appears on average at position (N − K)/(K + 1) + 1 = (N + 1)/(K + 1).</p>`,
          `<p>Why the gaps really are interchangeable: take any shuffle and swap the contents of two gaps (keeping the aces where the gap boundaries fall). The result is another equally likely shuffle with those two gap sizes exchanged. So all K + 1 gaps have the same distribution, not just the same mean, and their sizes add to N − K.</p>`,
          { q: 'Turning over a shuffled deck, at what position does the first heart appear on average?', a: 53 / 14, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '13 hearts split the 39 other cards into 14 gaps.', sol: '13 hearts split the 39 other cards into 14 gaps of average size 39/14. The first heart comes right after the first gap: 39/14 + 1 = 53/14 = (52 + 1)/(13 + 1) ≈ <b>3.79</b>.' },
          { id: 'a1', q: 'At what position does the last ace appear on average?', a: 4 * 53 / 5, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'By symmetry, count the same gap from the far end, or add four gaps and four aces.', sol: 'Before the last ace come four gaps and four aces: 4 × 48/5 + 4 = 4 × 53/5 = <b>42.4</b>. Or by reflection: from the far end, the last ace is the first ace, at average position 10.6 from that end, so 53 − 10.6 = 42.4.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Every ordering is equally likely', blocks: [
          `<p>If X₁, …, Xₙ are independent draws from the same continuous distribution (so no ties), all n! orderings are equally likely. That alone answers questions about ranks without knowing the distribution.</p>
           <p>The classic is <b>records</b>: the i-th value is a record (larger than everything before it) when it is the largest of the first i, which has probability 1/i. By linearity, the expected number of records among n is 1 + ½ + ⅓ + … + 1/n ≈ ln n + 0.58. In 10 years of annual rainfall you expect about 2.9 record years; in 1,000 years only about 7.5. Records thin out quickly even when nothing is changing, which is worth knowing before calling a run of records a trend.</p>`,
          { id: 'x2', q: 'Ten independent draws from the same continuous distribution. What is the expected number of records (values larger than all before them, counting the first)?', a: 7381 / 2520, tol: { abs: 0.005 }, hint: 'The i-th is a record with probability 1/i.', sol: '1 + ½ + ⅓ + … + 1/10 = 7381/2520 ≈ <b>2.93</b>.' },
          { worked: 'breaking a stick', q: 'A stick is broken at two uniformly random points. What is the probability the three pieces can form a triangle?', steps: [
            ['When do three lengths fail to make a triangle?', 'When one piece is at least as long as the other two together: longer than ½ of the stick.'],
            ['Can two pieces both be longer than ½?', 'No, so the three "too long" events can\'t overlap: P(fail) = 3 × P(a given piece > ½).'],
            ['P(the left piece > ½)?', 'Both break points must land in the right half: ¼. By symmetry, the same for each piece (the three pieces have the same distribution).'],
            'P(triangle) = 1 − 3 × ¼ = <b>¼</b>.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Symmetric thinking in a harder puzzle', blocks: [
          { worked: 'the plane seats', q: '100 passengers board a full plane. The first has lost their ticket and sits at random. Everyone after takes their own seat if free, otherwise a random free seat. What is the chance the last passenger gets their own seat?', steps: [
            ['What decides the outcome? Think about which seats matter.', 'Only seat 1 (the lost passenger\'s) and seat 100 (the last passenger\'s). As soon as anyone sits in one of those, the outcome is settled.'],
            ['When someone has to choose at random, how do those two seats compare?', 'They\'re both free and treated the same: whoever chooses is equally likely to pick either.'],
            'So seat 1 and seat 100 are equally likely to be taken first, and the last passenger gets their seat with probability <b>½</b>.',
          ] },
          { idea: 'When a calculation looks like it depends on 100 people, look for the two things that actually matter, and ask whether anything distinguishes them.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'Three people draw straws in turn from 3, one short. Is it better to draw first?', opts: [['No, each has a 1/3 chance'], ['Yes: the first has the most straws to choose from', 'More straws also means a smaller share of them are short.'], ['No, it\'s better to go last', 'The last draw has a 1/3 chance too.'], ['Yes: the chance falls with each draw', 'Work out the second person: 2/3 × 1/2.']], sol: 'Imagine laying out the straws in the order drawn: every position is equally likely to hold the short one. So the order doesn\'t matter.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Ask what can\'t matter: symmetry often gives the answer outright.', 'Three symmetries: relabel, reflect (k ↔ 7 − k, H ↔ n − H), reorder.', 'Ties break symmetry: (1 − P(tie))/2.', 'Write counts as sums of indicators.', 'K random special items among N: the first is at (N + 1)/(K + 1) on average.', 'Independent draws: every ordering equally likely, so expected records = 1 + ½ + … + 1/n.'],
      explain: { q: 'Give the one-sentence argument for why the first ace in a shuffled deck comes at position 53/5 on average.', a: 'The four aces split the 48 other cards into five gaps, which are interchangeable by symmetry, so each averages 48/5 cards, and the first ace comes right after the first gap: 48/5 + 1 = 53/5.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Two dice: what is the probability that the first shows more than the second?', a: 5 / 12, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'First, find the chance of a tie.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> symmetry plus the tie.</p><ol><li>P(tie) = 6/36 = 1/6.</li><li>Swapping the dice shows "first higher" and "second higher" are equally likely.</li><li>So P(first higher) = (1 − 1/6)/2 = <b>5/12</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check by counting:</b> of the 30 non-tied pairs, half (15) have the first higher: 15/36 = 5/12 ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: '5 fair flips: what is the probability of more heads than tails?', a: 0.5, hint: 'Can there be a tie?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> symmetry, with no room for a tie.</p><ol><li>With 5 flips, heads and tails can\'t be equal (5 is odd).</li><li>Swapping H and T makes "more heads" and "more tails" equally likely, and they cover everything.</li><li>So each is <b>½</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Contrast:</b> with 4 flips the tie takes 6/16 and the answer drops to 5/16.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: '20 balls go into 5 boxes at random. What is the expected number of empty boxes?', a: 5 * 0.8 ** 20, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: '(4/5)²⁰ for each box.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> indicator per box.</p><ol><li>One ball misses a given box with probability 4/5; all 20 miss it with probability 0.8²⁰ ≈ 0.0115.</li><li>Five boxes: 5 × 0.0115 ≈ <b>0.058</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Meaning:</b> with 4 balls per box on average, an empty box is rare: about one run in 17 has one.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'In a random ordering of 1 to 10, what is the expected number of positions where a number is larger than the one after it?', a: 4.5, hint: '9 adjacent pairs.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> indicator per adjacent pair.</p><ol><li>There are 9 adjacent pairs.</li><li>By symmetry each pair is in decreasing order with probability ½ (swapping the two numbers gives an equally likely ordering).</li><li>E = 9 × ½ = <b>4.5</b>.</li></ol><p>These positions are called descents; the indicators are dependent, but linearity doesn\'t care.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'In a shuffled deck, what is the expected number of adjacent pairs with the same colour?', a: 25, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '51 pairs. Given the first card\'s colour, what is the chance that the next matches?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> indicator per adjacent pair.</p><ol><li>There are 51 adjacent pairs.</li><li>Whatever the first card of a pair is, 25 of the other 51 cards share its colour, and by symmetry the next card is any of them equally: P(match) = 25/51.</li><li>E = 51 × 25/51 = <b>25</b>.</li></ol><p><b>The trap:</b> using ½ for each pair gives 25.5. Without replacement a match is slightly less likely than ½.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'A hand of 13 cards is dealt. What is the expected number of aces?', a: 1, hint: 'Each ace is in your hand with probability ¼.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> indicator per ace.</p><ol><li>The deck is dealt into 4 hands of 13, and by symmetry each ace is equally likely to be in any of them: ¼ for yours.</li><li>4 aces × ¼ = <b>1</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> the 4 hands share the 4 aces, so the average per hand must be 1.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'N ants sit on a 1-metre stick, each walking left or right at 1 m/min. When two meet, they both turn around. What is the longest possible time until all ants have fallen off, in minutes?', a: 1, hint: 'Ants are identical. What does a collision look like if you ignore which ant is which?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> stop tracking which ant is which.</p><ol><li>When two ants meet and both turn round, the picture (ants at those positions, moving those ways) is the same as if they had walked through each other.</li><li>So the set of positions evolves like N independent "ghost" ants walking straight, each falling off within 1 metre of walking.</li><li>The longest time is therefore <b>1 minute</b>, reached when an ant starts at an end walking the long way.</li></ol><p>Relabelling is the symmetry: the ants are interchangeable, so swapping their labels at each collision changes nothing you can observe.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'A round table has n seats, with n people sitting at random. Show that the expected number of people whose left neighbour is their friend, when each has exactly 2 friends in the room, is 2n/(n − 1).', sol: '<p><b>Proof with indicators:</b></p><ol><li>For each person, let I = 1 if their left neighbour is a friend.</li><li>By symmetry, a person\'s left neighbour is equally likely to be any of the other n − 1 people.</li><li>Two of those are friends, so P(I = 1) = 2/(n − 1).</li><li>Adding over n people: E = 2n/(n − 1).</li></ol><p>As n grows this tends to 2: each person\'s two friends between them sit to someone\'s left about twice per table.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Take a pack. Shuffle and record the position of the first ace 10 times, then the position of the first heart 10 times. Which is closer to its prediction of 10.6 or 3.79, and why?', sol: '<p>The heart is usually closer. The first-ace position has a standard deviation of about 8.2 cards, the first-heart position about 3.0, so ten trials pin down the heart\'s average roughly three times better (standard errors of about 2.6 against 1.0).</p><p><b>Point:</b> how quickly an average settles depends on the spread of what you\'re averaging, not just on the number of trials.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Puzzles topic', '#/topic/puzzles'], ['Classic puzzles', '#/bank/common'], ['Ants on a stick', '#/iq/wc-ants']],
    },

    recursion: {
      title: 'First-step analysis', minutes: 45,
      why: 'This is the single most reusable method in interview probability. If a problem has "keep going until…" in it, you almost certainly solve it by writing the answer in terms of itself.',
      goals: ['Write an equation for an unknown by conditioning on what happens first', 'Solve waiting-time and win-probability problems this way, with the right boundary conditions', 'Handle patterns of coin flips, including overlapping ones', 'Recurse on the size of a problem: from n to n − 1'],
      prereq: ['expectation', 'conditional', 'toolkit'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Condition on the first step', blocks: [
          `<p>Call the answer E (or p). Ask what can happen on the first step, and what the problem looks like afterwards. Often it looks like the original problem again, so E appears on both sides.</p>
           <p>It works for two kinds of question, and the equations look slightly different:</p>
           <ul><li><b>Expected waits or totals</b>: E = (cost of this step) + Σ P(next state) × E[from that state]. The "+1" for the step you just took is the most commonly forgotten term.</li>
           <li><b>Probabilities of ending somewhere</b>: p = Σ P(next state) × p[from that state], with no "+1", because a probability doesn't accumulate per step.</li></ul>
           <p>Then fix the <b>boundary conditions</b>: the values at states where the game is over. For an expected wait, E = 0 once you've finished; for a win probability, p = 1 at a win and 0 at a loss. With several states you get one equation per state, a small linear system. Solve by substitution, starting from the state nearest the end.</p>`,
          { fig: 'flow', alt: 'The recipe for first-step analysis.', steps: ['Name the unknown', 'List what can happen first', 'Say what the problem becomes', 'Write the equation', 'Solve'] },
          { worked: 'expected rolls for a six', q: 'How many rolls of a die do you expect to need to get a six?', steps: [
            ['Name the unknown.', 'E = the expected number of rolls.'],
            ['What can happen on the first roll?', 'A six, probability 1/6, and you\'re done. Otherwise, probability 5/6, and you\'re back at the start, with one roll used.'],
            ['Write the equation.', 'E = 1 + (5/6)E. The 1 is the roll you just used.'],
            'Solve: E/6 = 1, so E = 6.',
          ] },
          { q: 'A and B take turns rolling a die, A first. The first to roll a six wins. What is A\'s chance of winning?', a: 6 / 11, hint: 'If neither of the first two rolls is a six, A is back where she started: p = 1/6 + (5/6)(5/6)p.', sol: 'Condition on the first round of two rolls. A wins at once with probability 1/6. If A misses and B misses (probability 25/36), A is back at the start. So p = 1/6 + (25/36)p, giving p(11/36) = 1/6 and p = <b>6/11</b> ≈ 0.545. Going first is worth a 6 : 5 edge.' },
          { worked: 'a probability, not a wait', q: 'You flip a fair coin repeatedly. What is the probability you see two heads in a row before you see a tail?', steps: [
            ['States?', 'Start, and "one head so far". A tail at any point loses.'],
            ['From the start?', 'Heads (½) moves to "one head"; tails loses. p₀ = ½ p₁.'],
            ['From "one head"?', 'Heads wins; tails loses. p₁ = ½.'],
            'p₀ = ¼. Here there\'s no loop, so first-step analysis just multiplies along the path. It earns its keep when states can repeat.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Patterns of flips', blocks: [
          `<p>To find the expected wait for a pattern, track how much of it you've built so far. Each state gets an equation. Patterns that overlap with themselves (like HTH, which starts again with its own last H) take longer than ones that don't.</p>`,
          { fig: 'states', w: 420, h: 150, r: 22, nodes: { s: [50, 85, '0'], h: [170, 85, 'H'], hh: [290, 85, 'HH'], d: [390, 85, 'HHH', 'ok'] }, edges: [['s', 'h', '½'], ['h', 'hh', '½'], ['hh', 'd', '½'], ['h', 's', '½', 26], ['hh', 's', '½', 46]], loops: [['s', '½', 'top']], alt: 'States 0, H, HH and HHH. A head moves one step right; a tail returns to 0.', cap: 'Waiting for HHH: a head moves you right, and a tail always sends you back to the start.' },
          { worked: 'a self-overlapping pattern', q: 'What is the expected number of flips to see HTH?', steps: [
            ['What are the states?', 'Nothing useful (E₀), just seen H (E₁), just seen HT (E₂).'],
            ['From the start: heads goes to E₁, tails stays. Write it.', 'E₀ = 1 + ½E₁ + ½E₀, so E₀ = 2 + E₁.'],
            ['From H: heads keeps you at H (that H still counts), tails moves on.', 'E₁ = 1 + ½E₁ + ½E₂, so E₁ = 2 + E₂.'],
            ['From HT: heads finishes. What does tails do?', 'HTT has no useful ending, so back to the start: E₂ = 1 + ½ × 0 + ½E₀.'],
            'Substitute: E₀ = 2 + 2 + 1 + ½E₀, so E₀ = 10.',
          ] },
          { q: 'What is the expected number of fair coin flips until you first see HT?', a: 4, hint: 'Once you have an H, you only need a T: wait for an H (2 flips), then a T (2 more).', sol: 'Wait for the first H: 2 flips on average. From then on you are holding an H: another H keeps you holding one, and the first T completes HT. So you just wait for a T: 2 more flips. Total 2 + 2 = <b>4</b>. An H that follows an H doesn\'t lose progress.' },
          { q: 'And until HHH?', a: 14, hint: 'States 0, H, HH. A tail always sends you back to the start.', sol: 'States by progress: E₀, E₁ (one H), E₂ (HH). A tail always returns to the start. E₂ = 1 + ½ × 0 + ½E₀; E₁ = 1 + ½E₂ + ½E₀; E₀ = 1 + ½E₁ + ½E₀. From the first, E₀ = 2 + E₁. Put E₂ into the second: E₁ = 1 + ½(1 + ½E₀) + ½E₀ = 1.5 + ¾E₀. So E₀ = 3.5 + ¾E₀, giving E₀ = <b>14</b> = 2 + 4 + 8, the "2<sup>k+1</sup> − 2" pattern.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'Why is the wait for HH (6) longer than the wait for HT (4), when each is equally likely at any given spot?', opts: [['HH overlaps with itself'], ['H is rarer after H than T is', 'After a fair flip, H and T are equally likely.'], ['HH is a less likely pattern overall', 'Each has probability ¼ at any position.'], ['Flips aren\'t independent', 'They are.']], sol: 'After H, a tail in HH wastes everything, while a head in HT keeps your progress. Occurrences of HH bunch together, so it needs longer gaps between bunches to average the same rate.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Games that loop back', blocks: [
          `<p>Tennis from deuce, table tennis at 10–10: you need two points in a row, and a split returns you to the same state. Look at pairs of points: P(win) = p²/(p² + q²), ignoring the splits.</p>`,
          { fig: 'states', w: 400, h: 110, r: 26, nodes: { l: [50, 55, 'lose', 'bad'], d: [200, 55, 'deuce'], w: [350, 55, 'win', 'ok'] }, edges: [['d', 'w', 'p²'], ['d', 'l', 'q²']], alt: 'From deuce, you win with probability p squared, lose with q squared, and otherwise stay at deuce.', cap: 'Every two points, you win, lose, or return to deuce. Splits don\'t count, so only the ratio p² : q² matters.' },
          { q: 'You win each point with probability 2/3. From deuce, what is your probability of winning the game?', a: 4 / 5, hint: 'p² = 4/9 and q² = 1/9.', sol: 'Two points decide or return: win both (4/9), lose both (1/9), or split (4/9) and return to deuce. Ignoring the returns, P(win) = (4/9)/(4/9 + 1/9) = <b>4/5</b>. First-step form: P = 4/9 + (4/9)P, the same answer.' },
          `<p>Why "ignore the loops" works: from deuce, every two-point round ends the game with probability p² + q², and given that it ends, you won with probability p²/(p² + q²). The rounds that return to deuce change how long the game takes, not who wins it. This "condition on the step that ends things" shortcut applies to any game that resets.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'Recursing on the size of the problem', blocks: [
          `<p>The other kind of recursion goes from a problem of size n to smaller ones. Write the answer for n in terms of the answers for n − 1, n − 2, …, and build up from the smallest cases (this is dynamic programming).</p>
           <p>Example: you keep rolling a die and adding up the total. What is the chance the running total ever equals exactly n? Condition on the last roll before reaching n: to hit n you must have hit n − k for some k = 1…6 and then rolled k. So p<sub>n</sub> = (p<sub>n−1</sub> + … + p<sub>n−6</sub>)/6, with p₀ = 1 and p<sub>negative</sub> = 0.</p>
           <p>For large n, p<sub>n</sub> settles at 1/3.5 = 2/7: the totals you visit are spaced 3.5 apart on average, so you land on about 2 in every 7 numbers.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'Rolling a die and keeping a running total, what is the probability the total is ever exactly 3? (Give a decimal.)', a: 49 / 216, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'p₁ = 1/6, p₂ = (1 + p₁)/6, p₃ = (1 + p₁ + p₂)/6.', sol: 'p₁ = 1/6. p₂ = (p₀ + p₁)/6 = (1 + 1/6)/6 = 7/36. p₃ = (p₀ + p₁ + p₂)/6 = (1 + 1/6 + 7/36)/6 = (49/36)/6 = 49/216 ≈ <b>0.227</b>.' },
          { worked: 'counting by recursion', q: 'In how many ways can you climb 10 stairs taking 1 or 2 steps at a time?', steps: [
            ['Condition on the last move.', 'Your last move was 1 step (from stair 9) or 2 steps (from stair 8).'],
            'So W(n) = W(n − 1) + W(n − 2), with W(1) = 1, W(2) = 2.',
            'Building up: 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34, 55, <b>89</b>. The Fibonacci numbers.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Explore: race the patterns', blocks: [
          `<p>Simulate any pattern and compare with the exact answer. A neat rule: the expected wait is the sum of 2<sup>k</sup> over each length k at which the pattern's start matches its end. HTH: lengths 1 and 3, so 2 + 8 = 10.</p>`,
          { widget: 'pattern', predict: { q: 'Which takes longer on average to appear: HTT or HTH?', opts: ['HTH', 'HTT', 'They take the same time'], then: 'Pick each pattern and simulate it.' } },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Name the unknown, condition on the first step, and solve the equation. Waits get "+1" per step; probabilities don\'t.', 'Set the boundary values first: 0 left to wait once done; 1 at a win, 0 at a loss.', 'Patterns: one state per prefix you\'ve built.', 'Recurse on size: answer(n) from answer(n − 1), …, built up from the smallest case.', 'Self-overlapping patterns take longer (HTH: 10, HHH: 14, but HTT: 8).', 'Loops back to the same state: only the steps that end the game matter.'],
      explain: { q: 'In E = 1 + (5/6)E, what does each term mean?', a: 'E is the expected total. The 1 is the roll you must make regardless. (5/6)E says that with probability 5/6 you\'re in exactly the situation you started in, so you expect E more rolls. With probability 1/6 you\'re done, which adds nothing.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'What is the expected number of flips to see HH?', a: 6, hint: 'States 0 and H.', sol: '<p><b>States:</b> E₀ (no progress) and E₁ (just seen H).</p><ol><li>From the start: H moves to E₁, T stays: E₀ = 1 + ½E₁ + ½E₀, so E₀ = 2 + E₁.</li><li>From H: H finishes, T restarts: E₁ = 1 + ½ × 0 + ½E₀.</li><li>Substitute: E₀ = 2 + 1 + ½E₀, so ½E₀ = 3 and E₀ = <b>6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check with the overlap rule:</b> HH matches itself at lengths 1 and 2: 2 + 4 = 6 ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A coin has P(heads) = ⅓. What is the expected number of flips to see a head?', a: 3, hint: 'E = 1 + ⅔E.', sol: '<p><b>First step:</b> one flip; with probability ⅓ it is a head and you stop, with ⅔ you are back at the start.</p><ol><li>E = 1 + ⅔E.</li><li>⅓E = 1, so E = <b>3</b>.</li></ol><p>That\'s the geometric mean 1/p, derived from scratch.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A and B alternate flipping a fair coin, A first. The first head wins. What is A\'s chance?', a: 2 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'p = ½ + ¼p.', sol: '<p><b>First step over one round:</b> A wins at once with ½; both miss with ¼ and A is back where she started.</p><ol><li>p = ½ + ¼p.</li><li>¾p = ½, so p = <b>2/3</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> B\'s chance is ½ × 2/3 = 1/3 (B effectively goes first after A misses), and 2/3 + 1/3 = 1 ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'You win each point with probability 0.6. From deuce, what is your probability of winning?', a: 9 / 13, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: '0.36/(0.36 + 0.16).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> look at pairs of points and ignore the splits that return to deuce.</p><ol><li>Win both: 0.6² = 0.36. Lose both: 0.4² = 0.16.</li><li>P(win) = 0.36/(0.36 + 0.16) = 0.36/0.52 = <b>9/13</b> ≈ 0.692.</li></ol><p><b>Point:</b> a 60% edge per point becomes 69% from deuce. Needing to win by two amplifies a small edge, which is why the stronger player wins more games than points.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'What is the expected number of die rolls until you get two sixes in a row?', a: 42, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'States: 0 and "one six". E₀ = 1 + ⅙E₁ + ⅚E₀ and E₁ = 1 + ⅚E₀.', sol: '<p><b>States:</b> E₀ (last roll not a six, or start) and E₁ (last roll a six).</p><ol><li>E₀ = 1 + ⅙E₁ + ⅚E₀, so ⅙E₀ = 1 + ⅙E₁, i.e. E₀ = 6 + E₁.</li><li>E₁ = 1 + ⅙ × 0 + ⅚E₀.</li><li>E₀ = 6 + 1 + ⅚E₀, so ⅙E₀ = 7 and E₀ = <b>42</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check with the overlap rule</b> (for a fair die, sum of 6<sup>k</sup> over self-overlap lengths): 6 + 36 = 42 ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'You roll a die until two consecutive rolls match. What is the expected number of rolls?', a: 7, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'After the first roll, each roll matches the last with probability 1/6.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> after the first roll, every roll has a fresh 1/6 chance of matching the one before it.</p><ol><li>The first roll can\'t match anything: 1 roll.</li><li>Then you wait for a match with p = 1/6: 6 rolls on average.</li><li>Total 1 + 6 = <b>7</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Contrast</b> with two sixes in a row (42): there, a non-six destroys your progress, while here every roll sets up a new target.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'HHT and HTH race: flip a coin until one appears. What is the probability that HHT comes first?', a: 2 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'Wait for the first H. Then look at the next flips: HH… leads to HHT for sure; what about HT?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> first-step analysis from the moment you have an H (tails before that change nothing).</p><ol><li>Next flip H (½): you have HH. Now HHT must come first: more Hs keep you at HH, and the first T completes HHT. Win.</li><li>Next flip T (½): you have HT. Then H (½) completes HTH: lose. T (½) gives HTT, no useful ending: back to waiting for an H, probability p again.</li><li>p = ½ + ½ × ½ × p, so ¾p = ½ and p = <b>2/3</b>.</li></ol><p>Even though HTH (wait 10) and HHT (wait 8) differ in expected wait, races depend on how the patterns interact, not just on their waits.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Show that the expected number of flips to see k heads in a row is 2<sup>k+1</sup> − 2.', sol: '<p><b>Recursion on k:</b> let E<sub>k</sub> be the expected wait for k heads in a row.</p><ol><li>To get k in a row you must first get k − 1 in a row (E<sub>k−1</sub> flips on average).</li><li>Then one more flip: heads finishes; tails (½) sends you back to the start, needing E<sub>k</sub> more.</li><li>E<sub>k</sub> = E<sub>k−1</sub> + 1 + ½E<sub>k</sub>, so E<sub>k</sub> = 2E<sub>k−1</sub> + 2.</li><li>With E₀ = 0: 2, 6, 14, 30, … Check E<sub>k</sub> = 2<sup>k+1</sup> − 2 satisfies it: 2(2<sup>k</sup> − 2) + 2 = 2<sup>k+1</sup> − 2 ✓.</li></ol>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'With a real coin, count flips until HT, ten times. Then until HH, ten times. Do you see the 4 v 6 difference?', sol: '<p>You may not: the spread is large for ten trials. The HH wait has a standard deviation of about 4.7 flips and the HT wait 2 (two independent geometric waits, each with variance 2), so ten-trial averages have standard errors of about 1.5 and 0.6. You\'d need around 100 trials of each to separate 4 from 6 reliably.</p><p>That\'s worth feeling in your hands: an exact expected value can take a lot of data to see.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Expected value topic', '#/topic/ev'], ['HTH', '#/iq/sig-hth'], ['HHT v HTT', '#/iq/js-hht-htt'], ['Deuce', '#/iq/wc-deuce']],
    },

    markov: {
      title: 'Markov chains', minutes: 45,
      why: 'When a process has a state and rules for moving, a Markov chain turns a puzzle into a small set of equations. It\'s also how to think about regimes and order flow.',
      goals: ['Model a process as states with transition probabilities, and multiply matrices for n-step probabilities', 'Find stationary distributions and return times, and how fast a chain settles', 'Compute expected hitting times and absorption probabilities by first-step analysis on the states'],
      prereq: ['recursion'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'States and transitions', blocks: [
          `<p>A <b>Markov chain</b> moves between states, and where it goes next depends only on where it is now. Draw the states and arrows with probabilities, then look for structure: symmetric states can be merged.</p>
           <p>The "only on where it is now" part is a modelling choice, and often you can make it true by putting more into the state. Weather that depends on the last two days isn't Markov in "today's weather", but it is in the pair (yesterday, today). The waiting-for-HTH states in the last chapter work the same way: the state is "how much of the pattern you've built".</p>`,
          { fig: 'states', w: 360, h: 170, r: 28, nodes: { s: [100, 100, 'Sun', 'ok-soft'], r: [260, 100, 'Rain', 'acc-soft'] }, edges: [['s', 'r', '0.2', 22], ['r', 's', '0.4', 22]], loops: [['s', '0.8', 'top'], ['r', '0.6', 'top']], alt: 'Two states. Sun stays with 0.8 or moves to rain with 0.2. Rain stays with 0.6 or moves to sun with 0.4.', cap: 'Each state\'s outgoing arrows add to 1.' },
          `<p>The <b>stationary distribution</b> π is the long-run share of time in each state. If the chain is in balance, the flow out of a state equals the flow in. For two states this is one line: π<sub>S</sub> × P(S→R) = π<sub>R</sub> × P(R→S).</p>`,
          { widget: 'markov' },
          { q: 'Weather: a sunny day is followed by rain with probability 0.2; a rainy day by sun with probability 0.4. In the long run, what fraction of days are rainy?', a: 1 / 3, hint: 'In balance, the flow sunny → rainy equals the flow rainy → sunny.', sol: 'Balance the flows: π<sub>S</sub> × 0.2 = π<sub>R</sub> × 0.4, so π<sub>S</sub> = 2π<sub>R</sub>. With π<sub>S</sub> + π<sub>R</sub> = 1: π<sub>R</sub> = 0.2/(0.2 + 0.4) = <b>1/3</b>. Rain is "entered" half as easily as it\'s left, so it gets half as much time as sun.' },
          `<p><b>Several steps ahead.</b> Put the probabilities in a matrix P, with row i holding the chances of moving from state i. Then the n-step probabilities are the entries of Pⁿ: to go from i to k in two steps you go through some j, so P²<sub>ik</sub> = Σ<sub>j</sub> P<sub>ij</sub>P<sub>jk</sub>, which is matrix multiplication. A distribution over states, written as a row vector μ, moves to μP after one step; the stationary distribution is the one with πP = π.</p>
           <p><b>How fast it settles.</b> For a two-state chain with switching probabilities a and b, the gap between today's distribution and the long run shrinks by a factor of 1 − a − b each step. For the weather, 1 − 0.2 − 0.4 = 0.4: after three days a forecast is already within 0.4³ ≈ 6% of the long-run odds. That factor is the matrix's second eigenvalue (the first is always 1), which is the link to the linear algebra chapter.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'In the weather chain, it is sunny today. What is the probability it rains the day after tomorrow?', a: 0.28, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Two routes: sun then rain, or rain then rain.', sol: 'Sun → sun → rain: 0.8 × 0.2 = 0.16. Sun → rain → rain: 0.2 × 0.6 = 0.12. Total <b>0.28</b>, the (S, R) entry of P². Already close to the long-run 1/3.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Return times', blocks: [
          `<p>In the long run, if the chain spends a fraction π of its time in a state, it returns to that state every 1/π steps on average. For a random walk on a graph, π is proportional to each node's number of connections, which makes return times easy.</p>
           <p>Why degree? A walker at node i uses each of its d<sub>i</sub> edges with probability 1/d<sub>i</sub>. Try π<sub>i</sub> = d<sub>i</sub>/(2 × number of edges). Then the flow along any edge, π<sub>i</sub> × 1/d<sub>i</sub> = 1/(2E), is the same in both directions. Flows that balance edge by edge (<b>detailed balance</b>) certainly balance overall, so this π is stationary. The return time to node i is 1/π<sub>i</sub> = 2E/d<sub>i</sub>.</p>`,
          { q: 'In the weather chain, starting on a sunny day, how many days until the next sunny day, on average?', a: 1.5, hint: 'π<sub>S</sub> = 2/3.', sol: 'Sun takes up π<sub>S</sub> = 2/3 of days, so visits are 1/π<sub>S</sub> = <b>1.5</b> days apart on average. Check by first step: tomorrow is sunny (0.8, 1 day) or rainy (0.2), and from rain the wait for sun is geometric with mean 1/0.4 = 2.5: 0.8 × 1 + 0.2 × (1 + 2.5) = 1.5 ✓.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Hitting times', blocks: [
          `<p>To find the expected time to reach a target, write h<sub>i</sub> = 1 + Σ P(i → j) h<sub>j</sub> for every state i (with h = 0 at the target) and solve. Symmetry keeps the system small.</p>`,
          { q: 'A walker on positions 0, 1, 2, 3 moves left or right with probability ½ each, except that from 0 it always moves to 1. Starting at 0, what is the expected number of steps to reach 3?', a: 9, hint: 'h₃ = 0, h₀ = 1 + h₁, and h<sub>i</sub> = 1 + ½h<sub>i−1</sub> + ½h<sub>i+1</sub> for i = 1, 2.', sol: 'Equations: h₀ = 1 + h₁; h₁ = 1 + ½h₀ + ½h₂; h₂ = 1 + ½h₁ + ½ × 0. Substitute h₀ into the second: h₁ = 1 + ½(1 + h₁) + ½h₂, so h₁ = 3 + h₂. Then h₂ = 1 + ½(3 + h₂), giving h₂ = 5, h₁ = 8, h₀ = <b>9</b>. In general n² steps to reach n from a reflecting wall.' },
          { fig: 'states', w: 420, h: 140, r: 22, nodes: { a: [50, 80, 'd=3'], b: [160, 80, 'd=2'], c: [270, 80, 'd=1'], t: [375, 80, 'goal', 'ok'] }, edges: [['a', 'b', '1'], ['b', 'c', '⅔', 18], ['c', 'b', '⅔', 18], ['b', 'a', '⅓', 34], ['c', 't', '⅓']], alt: 'Corners of a cube merged by distance from the goal: 3, 2, 1 and 0.', cap: 'A walk on a cube\'s corners. Merging corners by distance from the goal turns 8 states into 4.' },
          { worked: 'the walk on a cube', q: 'A bug walks randomly along the edges of a cube. From the opposite corner, what is the expected number of moves to reach the goal corner?', steps: [
            ['Merge corners. What matters about a corner?', 'Only its distance from the goal, 3, 2, 1 or 0.'],
            ['From distance 3, where can it go?', 'All 3 edges lead to distance 2: h₃ = 1 + h₂.'],
            ['From distance 2?', 'Two edges lead closer, and one back: h₂ = 1 + ⅔h₁ + ⅓h₃.'],
            ['From distance 1?', 'One edge hits the goal, and two go back: h₁ = 1 + ⅔h₂.'],
            'Substitute: h₂ = 1 + ⅔(1 + ⅔h₂) + ⅓(1 + h₂) = 2 + (7/9)h₂, so h₂ = 9, h₁ = 7 and h₃ = <b>10</b>.',
          ] },
          `<p><b>Absorption probabilities</b> use the same equations without the "+1". If some states end the process (absorbing states), the chance of ending in a particular one satisfies a<sub>i</sub> = Σ P(i → j) a<sub>j</sub>, with a = 1 in that state and 0 in the other absorbing ones. Gambler's ruin, the next chapter, is the most famous case.</p>`,
          { worked: 'which end?', q: 'States 0, 1, 2, 3. From 1 and 2 you move one step left or right with probability ½ each; 0 and 3 are absorbing. Starting at 1, what is the chance of ending at 3?', steps: [
            ['Unknowns and boundaries?', 'a₁ and a₂, with a₀ = 0 and a₃ = 1.'],
            ['Equations?', 'a₁ = ½a₀ + ½a₂ = ½a₂; a₂ = ½a₁ + ½a₃ = ½a₁ + ½.'],
            'Substitute: a₁ = ½(½a₁ + ½), so ¾a₁ = ¼ and a₁ = <b>1/3</b>: the start position over the length, as gambler\'s ruin will say in general.',
          ] },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'A price is modelled as being in a "trending" or "calm" regime, each with its own daily behaviour. What does the Markov assumption say?', opts: [['Tomorrow depends only on today'], ['Prices never trend for long', 'It says nothing about the length, which depends on the stay probabilities.'], ['Tomorrow\'s regime is independent of today\'s', 'That would be no chain at all.'], ['Regimes last exactly one day', 'A high chance of staying gives long regimes.']], sol: 'The model has no memory beyond the current state. A stay probability of s gives regimes that last 1/(1 − s) days on average.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Next state depends only on the current one; put more in the state if it doesn\'t.', 'n-step probabilities are matrix powers; a two-state chain forgets its start at rate 1 − a − b per step.', 'Stationary distribution: balance the flows; return time = 1/π.', 'Absorption: a<sub>i</sub> = Σ P a<sub>j</sub> with 1 and 0 at the ends (no "+1").', 'Hitting times: one equation per state, h = 1 + Σ P h.', 'Merge symmetric states before solving.', 'Random walk on a graph: π is proportional to degree.'],
      explain: { q: 'What is a stationary distribution, in plain words, and why is the return time 1/π?', a: 'It\'s the pattern of time spent in each state that the chain settles into, and that doesn\'t change from one step to the next. If you spend a fraction π of your time in a state, the visits must be 1/π steps apart on average, as visits per step and steps per visit are reciprocals.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A two-state chain moves A → B with probability 0.1 and B → A with probability 0.3. What is the long-run share of time in B?', a: 0.25, hint: 'a/(a + b).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> balance the flows between the two states.</p><ol><li>π<sub>A</sub> × 0.1 = π<sub>B</sub> × 0.3, so π<sub>A</sub> = 3π<sub>B</sub>.</li><li>π<sub>A</sub> + π<sub>B</sub> = 1 ⇒ 4π<sub>B</sub> = 1 ⇒ π<sub>B</sub> = <b>0.25</b>.</li></ol><p><b>General formula:</b> with switching probabilities a (into B) and b (out of B), π<sub>B</sub> = a/(a + b) = 0.1/0.4. B is easy to leave and hard to enter, so it gets little time.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'In that chain, what is the expected return time to B?', a: 4, hint: '1/π.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> return time = 1/π.</p><ol><li>π<sub>B</sub> = 0.25, so visits to B are 1/0.25 = <b>4</b> steps apart on average.</li></ol><p><b>Check by first step:</b> from B, stay with 0.7 (1 step); leave with 0.3, then wait for A → B, geometric with mean 1/0.1 = 10. So 0.7 × 1 + 0.3 × (1 + 10) = 0.7 + 3.3 = 4 ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A chain has states A, B, C. From A it always moves to B; from C it always moves to B; from B it moves to A or C with probability ½ each. What is the long-run share of time in B?', a: 0.5, hint: 'It\'s in B every other step.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> look at the pattern of visits.</p><ol><li>Every step from A or C goes to B, and every step from B leaves it. So the chain alternates: B, (A or C), B, (A or C), …</li><li>It is in B every other step: <b>½</b>. A and C share the other half, ¼ each.</li></ol><p><b>Check with degrees:</b> this is a walk on the path A–B–C, with degrees 1, 2, 1. π ∝ degree gives ¼, ½, ¼ ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A regime has a stay probability of 0.9. What is its expected length, in days?', a: 10, hint: 'Geometric.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> each day the regime ends with probability 0.1, independently, so its length is geometric.</p><ol><li>Mean = 1/(1 − 0.9) = 1/0.1 = <b>10</b> days.</li></ol><p><b>Note:</b> geometric lengths are skewed: the median is only about 7 days (0.9⁷ ≈ 0.48), while a few regimes last 30 days or more.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'A knight starts in a corner of a chessboard and moves at random. What is the expected number of moves until it returns to that corner?', a: 168, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Return time = (sum of all degrees)/(degree of the corner). Squares have 2, 3, 4, 6 or 8 knight moves, and they total 336.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> a random walk on a graph has π<sub>i</sub> = d<sub>i</sub>/Σd, so the return time is Σd/d<sub>i</sub>.</p><ol><li>Squares are the nodes and knight moves the edges. Count each square\'s moves: 4 corners have 2; 8 squares next to corners on the edge have 3; 20 more have 4 (the rest of the edges and the squares diagonal to corners); 16 have 6; 16 central squares have 8.</li><li>Total: 4 × 2 + 8 × 3 + 20 × 4 + 16 × 6 + 16 × 8 = 8 + 24 + 80 + 96 + 128 = 336.</li><li>Return time to a corner: 336/2 = <b>168</b> moves.</li></ol><p>No need to solve 64 equations: the degree formula does it.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'A walker on positions 0 to 4 moves ±1 with equal chance, with a reflecting wall at 0. What is the expected number of steps from 0 to 4?', a: 16, hint: 'The n² rule, or solve the equations.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> add the expected times to go up one level at a time.</p><ol><li>From i to i + 1 takes 2i + 1 steps on average (see the stretch question): 1, 3, 5, 7 for i = 0…3.</li><li>Total: 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 = <b>16</b> = 4².</li></ol><p><b>Point:</b> a fair walk covers distance n in about n² steps. Random motion is slow: twice as far takes four times as long.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'A frog hops between three lily pads in a triangle, moving to each of the other two with equal chance. Starting on pad A, what is the expected number of hops to reach pad B?', a: 2, hint: 'Each hop lands on B with probability ½.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> first step. From A or C (the only other pad), each hop lands on B with probability ½.</p><ol><li>E = 1 + ½ × 0 + ½E, so E = <b>2</b>.</li></ol><p>It\'s a geometric wait with p = ½. The triangle\'s symmetry means A and C are the same "state" as far as reaching B goes.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Derive the n² rule: for a fair walk on 0…n with a reflecting wall at 0, show that the expected time to go from i to i + 1 is 2i + 1, and add them up.', sol: '<p><b>Proof:</b> let t<sub>i</sub> be the expected time to go from i to i + 1.</p><ol><li>From 0 the walker must step to 1: t₀ = 1.</li><li>From i ≥ 1: one step, then either you\'re at i + 1 (½) or at i − 1 (½), from where you need t<sub>i−1</sub> to get back to i and then t<sub>i</sub> more. So t<sub>i</sub> = 1 + ½(t<sub>i−1</sub> + t<sub>i</sub>).</li><li>Rearranging: t<sub>i</sub> = 2 + t<sub>i−1</sub>, so t<sub>i</sub> = 2i + 1.</li><li>From 0 to n: Σ<sub>i=0</sub><sup>n−1</sup> (2i + 1) = n², the sum of the first n odd numbers.</li></ol>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Classify the last 60 trading days of an index as Up or Down. Count the four kinds of transitions and estimate P(up after up) and P(up after down). Are they different? What would you expect from noise?', sol: '<p>With about 30 days after ups and 30 after downs, each estimated probability has a standard error of about √(0.25/30) ≈ 0.09, so the difference of the two has a standard error of about 0.13. A gap under about 0.25 is consistent with noise.</p><p>Daily index direction has very little persistence, so expect a small gap. You\'ll formalise this test in the hypothesis testing chapter.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Markov chains topic', '#/topic/markov'], ['Cube walk', '#/iq/wc-cube-walk'], ['Random rook', '#/iq/drw-rook']],
    },

    walks: {
      title: 'Random walks and martingales', minutes: 50,
      why: 'A trader\'s P&L is a random walk with a small drift. This chapter tells you how likely you are to hit a target before a stop, how long it takes, and why no betting system can turn a fair game into a winning one.',
      goals: ['Solve gambler\'s ruin, fair and biased, including against an unlimited opponent', 'Use the martingale trick: a fair game\'s expected value never changes', 'Find expected durations with Wald\'s identity and the i(N − i) formula', 'Use the reflection principle and the ballot theorem', 'Know how far a random walk wanders: about √n'],
      prereq: ['recursion', 'markov'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Fair games are martingales', blocks: [
          `<p>A <b>martingale</b> is a process whose expected next value equals its current value. If each step has expected change zero, your expected position at any sensible stopping time is where you started. For a fair ±1 walk from i that stops at 0 or N:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">i = N × P(reach N) + 0 × P(reach 0), so P(reach N) = i/N</div>
           <p>The same answer comes from first-step analysis, which is a good check. Let p<sub>i</sub> be the chance of reaching N from i. Then p<sub>i</sub> = ½p<sub>i−1</sub> + ½p<sub>i+1</sub>, with p₀ = 0 and p<sub>N</sub> = 1. That says each p<sub>i</sub> is the average of its neighbours, so the p<sub>i</sub> lie on a straight line from 0 to 1: p<sub>i</sub> = i/N.</p>
           <p>The fine print: "sensible stopping time" means a rule that decides to stop using only what has happened so far (a <b>stopping time</b>), and the game must not be able to run up unbounded wealth or debt before stopping. Between two barriers both hold. The doubling strategy below breaks the second condition, which is the only reason it seems to work.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 30], y: [0, 10], xl: 'steps', yl: 'wealth', xt: [0, 10, 20, 30], yt: [0, 3, 10], hlines: [{ y: 10, t: 'target', tone: 'ok' }, { y: 0, tone: 'bad' }], series: [{ step: false, pts: [3, 4, 3, 4, 5, 4, 5, 6, 5, 6, 7, 6, 7, 8, 7, 8, 9, 8, 9, 10].map((v, i) => [i, v]), tone: 'ok' }, { pts: [3, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 0].map((v, i) => [i, v]), tone: 'bad' }], alt: 'Two walks from 3: one reaches 10, the other reaches 0.', cap: 'Two walks from 3. Most walks from here end at 0, but those that win, win more. The expected final wealth is still 3.' },
          { widget: 'walk', predict: { q: 'Start at $4, stop at $0 or $10, with a fair coin. What share of walks reach $10?', opts: ['About 40%', 'About 50%', 'About 16%', 'About 60%'], then: 'Run the simulation with Start 4 and Top 10. Then try a small bias, like 0.45.' } },
          { q: 'You have $3 and bet $1 on fair coin flips until you have $10 or nothing. What is the probability you reach $10?', a: 0.3, hint: 'Your expected wealth stays at 3.', sol: 'A fair game keeps expected wealth at 3, and the game ends at 10 or 0: 3 = 10 × P + 0 × (1 − P), so P = 3/10 = <b>0.3</b>. You\'re usually ruined, but the wins are big enough to keep the average at $3.' },
          { q: 'How many flips does that game last on average?', a: 21, hint: 'For a fair walk between barriers, the expected duration is i(N − i).', sol: 'The expected duration of a fair walk from i between 0 and N is i(N − i) (derived in the stretch homework): 3 × 7 = <b>21</b> flips. It\'s longest from the middle (5 × 5 = 25).' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'You bet $1 on a fair coin, double after each loss, and stop after your first win. Doesn\'t that guarantee +$1?', opts: [['No: a rare huge loss cancels the wins'], ['Yes, as long as you start small', 'You win $1 most of the time, and lose a fortune occasionally.'], ['Yes, because a win must come eventually', 'Only with unlimited money and no bet limit.'], ['No, because the coin remembers your losses', 'The coin has no memory.']], sol: 'A fair game stays fair for any stopping rule that can\'t see the future, as long as your bets are bounded. Doubling swaps many small wins for one rare catastrophe, and the expected profit stays exactly zero.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Biased walks', blocks: [
          `<p>If each step is +1 with probability p and −1 with q = 1 − p, let r = q/p. Then</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">P(reach N before 0, starting from i) = (1 − r<sup>i</sup>)/(1 − r<sup>N</sup>)</div>
           <p>The quantity r<sup>X</sup> is a martingale, which is where this comes from.</p>`,
          { proof: 'E[r<sup>X+step</sup>] = r<sup>X</sup>(p·r + q/r) = r<sup>X</sup>(q + p) = r<sup>X</sup>. So r<sup>X</sup> keeps its expected value. At the stop: r<sup>i</sup> = P × r<sup>N</sup> + (1 − P) × 1. Solve for P.' },
          { q: 'Steps are +1 with probability 0.6. Starting at 2, what is the probability of reaching 4 before 0?', a: 9 / 13, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'r = 0.4/0.6 = 2/3.', sol: 'r = q/p = 2/3. P = (1 − r²)/(1 − r⁴) = (1 − 4/9)/(1 − 16/81) = (5/9)/(65/81) = <b>9/13</b> ≈ 0.69. (Simplify: (1 − r²)/(1 − r⁴) = 1/(1 + r²) = 1/(1 + 4/9).) Same as the deuce answer with p = 0.6: deuce is this walk, two points either side.' },
          `<p><b>Against an unlimited opponent.</b> Let N → ∞. If the walk drifts towards you (p > q, so r < 1), r<sup>N</sup> → 0 and the chance of never being ruined from i is 1 − r<sup>i</sup>. If it is fair or against you, r<sup>N</sup> → ∞ (or the fair formula i/N → 0) and ruin is <b>certain</b>. That's the casino's position: against a house with effectively unlimited money and an edge, a finite gambler is eventually ruined with probability 1.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'Steps are +1 with probability 0.6 and −1 with 0.4, with no upper target. Starting at 2, what is the probability of ever hitting 0?', a: 4 / 9, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'P(ruin) = r<sup>i</sup> with r = q/p.', sol: 'r = 0.4/0.6 = 2/3, so P(ruin) = r² = <b>4/9</b> ≈ 0.44. Even with a good edge, starting only 2 units from ruin is dangerous: capital buys survival.' },
          { market: 'A tiny edge compounds. At p = 0.51, starting with $100 and aiming for $200, r = 0.96 and you reach the target with probability about 98%. At p = 0.49 it\'s about 2%. Over many trades, edge dominates luck, as long as you can\'t be knocked out first.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Wald\'s identity', blocks: [
          `<p>For a walk with average step μ, stopped at a time T that doesn't peek into the future, E[position at T] = μ × E[T]. If the walk hits its target exactly (steps of ±1), that gives E[T] directly.</p>
           <p>Why it holds: the position at T is the sum of the steps up to T. Step k is taken only if T ≥ k, and whether T ≥ k is decided before step k happens, so the step is independent of that decision. So E[sum] = Σ<sub>k</sub> μ P(T ≥ k) = μ E[T], using the tail-sum formula. For a fair walk (μ = 0) Wald gives 0 = 0, so durations need the second martingale, S² − n, in the stretch homework.</p>`,
          { q: 'A walk steps +1 with probability 0.6 and −1 with 0.4, starting at 0. What is the expected number of steps to first reach +5?', a: 25, hint: 'The drift is 0.6 − 0.4 per step.', sol: 'The drift is μ = 0.6 − 0.4 = 0.2 per step. Steps are ±1, so the walk lands on +5 exactly when it stops: Wald gives 5 = 0.2 × E[T], so E[T] = <b>25</b> steps. (It\'s certain to get there, because the drift is upwards.)' },
          `<p><b>How far does a walk wander?</b> A fair ±1 walk after n steps has mean 0 and variance n, so its typical distance from the start is about √n, and by the central limit theorem its position is roughly normal: E|S<sub>n</sub>| ≈ √(2n/π) ≈ 0.8√n. After 100 steps you are typically about 8 away. That √n is the same √time that scales volatility, and it's why the walk takes about n² steps to travel distance n.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'The reflection principle', blocks: [
          `<p>How likely is a fair walk to touch level a within n steps? Take any path that touches a and ends below it. Reflect everything after the first touch: it's now a path that ends above a, equally likely. So paths touching a split evenly above and below:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">P(touch a by step n) = 2 P(end above a) + P(end at a)</div>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 14], y: [-1, 7], xl: 'steps', xt: [0, 7, 14], yt: [0, 3], hlines: [{ y: 3, t: 'level a', tone: 'acc' }], series: [{ pts: PATH.map((v, i) => [i, v]), tone: 'acc' }, { pts: PATH.slice(3).map((v, i) => [i + 3, 6 - v]), tone: 'bad', dash: true }], alt: 'A path touching level 3, and its mirror image above the level.', cap: 'After the first touch, the mirror-image path is just as likely, so each path below has a twin above.' },
          { id: 'a1', q: 'A fair ±1 walk runs for 4 steps. What is the probability it touches +2 at some point?', a: 3 / 8, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'P(S₄ = 4) = 1/16 and P(S₄ = 2) = 4/16.', sol: '2 × 1/16 + 4/16 = <b>3/8</b>.' },
          { market: 'This is why a stop-loss gets hit more often than people expect: a price is about twice as likely to touch a level at some time as to finish beyond it.' },
          `<p><b>The ballot theorem.</b> In an election A gets a votes and B gets b < a, counted in random order. The chance that A is strictly ahead throughout the count is (a − b)/(a + b). The proof is reflection again: bad counts that start with a vote for A must touch level 0 later; reflecting their start up to the first tie turns each into a count starting with a vote for B, one to one. So bad counts are twice the counts that start with B, which happens with probability b/(a + b): P(good) = 1 − 2b/(a + b) = (a − b)/(a + b).</p>`,
          { id: 'x2', q: 'A gets 6 votes and B gets 4, counted in a random order. What is the probability A is strictly ahead after every vote counted?', a: 0.2, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: '(a − b)/(a + b).', sol: '(6 − 4)/(6 + 4) = <b>0.2</b>. Even with a 60–40 win, A trails or is tied at some point 80% of the time.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Fair game: expected value at the stop = start, so P(reach N) = i/N.', 'Fair walk duration between barriers: i(N − i).', 'Biased: (1 − r<sup>i</sup>)/(1 − r<sup>N</sup>) with r = q/p; against unlimited money, ruin is r<sup>i</sup> with an edge and certain without.', 'A walk wanders about √n in n steps.', 'Ballot: A always ahead with probability (a − b)/(a + b).', 'Wald: target = drift × expected time.', 'Touching a level is about twice as likely as ending beyond it.', 'No stopping rule makes a fair game profitable.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why can\'t a clever betting system beat a fair game?', a: 'Each bet has expected profit zero whatever you\'ve done before, since the coin doesn\'t know your history. A sum of zero-expectation bets has zero expectation, for any rule that decides based on the past. Systems reshape the distribution, trading frequent small wins for rare large losses, but they can\'t move its mean.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'You start with $5, and bet $1 at a time on fair flips until you have $20 or nothing. What is the probability of reaching $20?', a: 0.25, hint: 'i/N.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> a fair game keeps expected wealth constant.</p><ol><li>5 = 20 × P(reach 20) + 0 × P(ruin).</li><li>P = 5/20 = <b>0.25</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Interpretation:</b> to quadruple your money in a fair game you must accept a 75% chance of losing it all. Fair games don\'t make money; they only rearrange risk.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'How long does that game last on average?', a: 75, hint: 'i(N − i).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> fair walk between barriers lasts i(N − i) on average.</p><ol><li>i = 5, N − i = 15.</li><li>5 × 15 = <b>75</b> flips.</li></ol><p><b>Check the shape:</b> from the middle ($10) it would be 100 flips, the maximum. Starting near a barrier makes the game short, usually because you lose quickly.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'p = 0.55. Starting at 5, what is the probability of reaching 10 before 0?', a: (1 - (9 / 11) ** 5) / (1 - (9 / 11) ** 10), tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'r = 9/11. It simplifies to 1/(1 + r⁵).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> biased ruin with r = q/p = 0.45/0.55 = 9/11.</p><ol><li>P = (1 − r⁵)/(1 − r¹⁰). Since 1 − r¹⁰ = (1 − r⁵)(1 + r⁵), this is 1/(1 + r⁵).</li><li>r⁵ = (9/11)⁵ ≈ 0.367.</li><li>P = 1/1.367 ≈ <b>0.732</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Compare:</b> a fair coin gives 0.5. A 55% edge per bet turns into 73% over a 5-unit race: small edges compound.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A walk has drift +0.1 a step. What is the expected time to first reach +20?', a: 200, hint: 'Wald.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> Wald\'s identity, E[position at T] = drift × E[T].</p><ol><li>With ±1 steps the walk is exactly at +20 when it stops.</li><li>20 = 0.1 × E[T], so E[T] = <b>200</b> steps.</li></ol><p><b>Sense check:</b> on average the walk gains 0.1 a step, so 200 steps gain 20.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'At p = 0.49, starting with 50 and betting 1 at a time, what is the chance you double your money before going broke?', a: 1 / (1 + (51 / 49) ** 50), tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'r = 51/49. Start halfway: 1/(1 + r⁵⁰).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> biased ruin with i = 50, N = 100, r = 0.51/0.49 = 51/49.</p><ol><li>Starting halfway, P = 1/(1 + r⁵⁰).</li><li>r⁵⁰ = e<sup>50 ln(51/49)</sup> ≈ e<sup>50 × 0.04</sup> = e² ≈ 7.4.</li><li>P ≈ 1/8.4 ≈ <b>0.119</b>.</li></ol><p><b>The lesson:</b> a 49% coin looks almost fair, yet over a long game it wins only 12% of the time instead of 50%. Casinos rely on exactly this, and it\'s why a trader with no edge who pays costs is slowly ground down.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'A fair ±1 walk runs for 6 steps. What is the probability that it touches +2?', a: 2 * (6 + 1) / 64 + 15 / 64, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'P(S₆ = 4 or 6) = 7/64 and P(S₆ = 2) = 15/64.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> reflection: P(touch a) = 2P(end above a) + P(end at a).</p><ol><li>Ending at 6 needs 6 ups: 1 way. At 4: 5 ups, C(6, 5) = 6 ways. So P(end above 2) = 7/64.</li><li>Ending at 2: 4 ups, C(6, 4) = 15 ways: 15/64.</li><li>P(touch 2) = 2 × 7/64 + 15/64 = <b>29/64</b> ≈ 0.453.</li></ol><p><b>Compare:</b> the chance of finishing at 2 or above is 22/64 ≈ 0.34. Touching is much more likely than finishing there.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'A stock with no drift has 1% daily volatility. Roughly what is the chance that it touches −5% at some point in 25 days? Use the normal and reflection.', a: 2 * 0.15866, tol: { abs: 0.02 }, hint: 'Over 25 days the sd is 5%, so −5% is one sd. What is P(end below −1σ)?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> scale the volatility, then reflect.</p><ol><li>25-day volatility: 1% × √25 = 5%, so −5% is one standard deviation down.</li><li>P(end below −1σ) ≈ P(Z &lt; −1) ≈ 0.159.</li><li>Reflection: P(touch) ≈ 2 × 0.159 ≈ <b>0.32</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Practical point:</b> a stop-loss one standard deviation away is hit about one time in three over the period, twice as often as the price ends below it. Set stops with that in mind.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Show that S<sub>n</sub>² − n is a martingale for a fair ±1 walk, and use it to derive the i(N − i) duration formula.', sol: '<p><b>Martingale:</b> given S<sub>n</sub>, the next value is S<sub>n</sub> ± 1, so E[S<sub>n+1</sub>²] = ½(S<sub>n</sub> + 1)² + ½(S<sub>n</sub> − 1)² = S<sub>n</sub>² + 1. Subtracting the extra 1 each step: E[S<sub>n+1</sub>² − (n + 1)] = S<sub>n</sub>² − n.</p><p><b>Duration:</b> start at i and stop at T (hitting 0 or N). Optional stopping gives i² = E[S<sub>T</sub>²] − E[T]. S<sub>T</sub> is N with probability i/N and 0 otherwise, so E[S<sub>T</sub>²] = N² × i/N = iN. Hence E[T] = iN − i² = <b>i(N − i)</b>.</p><p>Two martingales, S and S² − n, give the two classic answers: where the walk ends and how long it takes.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Play the $3 to $10 game with a coin, ten times over. Record wins and lengths. Then open the Bet sizing game in the app. Does a small edge feel as strong as the maths says?', sol: '<p>Expect about 3 wins in 10 and an average length near 21, with a large spread (some games end in 3 flips, others last 60+).</p><p>Over a few games, a small edge is invisible: luck dominates. Over hundreds, the edge dominates. Feeling that difference is the point: judge a strategy by its edge and its sample size, never by a handful of outcomes.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Random walks topic', '#/topic/walks'], ['Gambler\'s ruin', '#/iq/fr-ruin-6-4'], ['Walk to −100 or +50', '#/iq/imc-walk']],
    },

    stopping: {
      title: 'Optimal stopping and dynamic programming', minutes: 45,
      why: 'When should you stop and take what you have? Dynamic programming solves it by starting from the end, and the same logic prices American options and decides when to close a position.',
      goals: ['Solve stopping games by backward induction, writing the value as V = E[max(stop, continue)]', 'Set thresholds: stop when what you have beats what continuing is worth', 'Value options to continue, including costly ones', 'Solve games whose state changes, such as the red-and-black card game', 'Use the 37% rule'],
      prereq: ['expectation', 'recursion'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Work backwards', blocks: [
          `<p>In a game with a limited number of chances, solve the last decision first. Its value tells you the threshold for the decision before it. In "roll a die, reroll if you like, keep the last roll": a reroll is worth 3.5, so keep 4, 5 and 6.</p>
           <p>Written as a formula, the value with n chances left is</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">V<sub>n</sub> = E[ max(X, V<sub>n−1</sub>) ], with V₀ = what you get if you can't continue</div>
           <p>Read it as: draw X, then take whichever is better, keeping X or the value of carrying on. This is the <b>Bellman equation</b>, and every problem in this chapter is a version of it. The max inside the expectation is the whole point: you decide after you see X, and that option is what makes the game worth more than E[X].</p>
           <p>Two features to notice. The threshold rises with the number of chances left (more chances, pickier you can be). And each extra chance adds less than the one before, because you can only improve on an already-good position by so much.</p>`,
          { fig: 'bars', vals: [3.5, 3.5, 3.5, 4, 5, 6], max: 6.5, labs: ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6'], show: ['3.5', '3.5', '3.5', '4', '5', '6'], tone: (i) => (i > 2 ? 'ok' : ''), w: 320, h: 190, xl: 'first roll', alt: 'For a first roll of 1, 2 or 3, rerolling is worth 3.5. For 4, 5 or 6, keep it.', cap: 'What each first roll is worth if you play well: reroll the low ones, and keep the rest. The average of these bars is the game\'s value.' },
          { q: 'With one reroll allowed, what is the game worth?', a: 4.25, hint: 'Keep 4–6; reroll otherwise for 3.5.', sol: 'A reroll is worth 3.5, so keep 4, 5 and 6 and reroll 1, 2 and 3. Value = E[max(X, 3.5)] = (3.5 + 3.5 + 3.5 + 4 + 5 + 6)/6 = (4 + 5 + 6)/6 + (3/6) × 3.5 = 2.5 + 1.75 = <b>4.25</b>.' },
          { q: 'With two rerolls?', a: 14 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'Continuing is now worth 4.25, so keep only 5 and 6.', sol: 'With two rerolls, continuing after the first roll is worth the one-reroll game, 4.25. Keep only 5 and 6 (both beat 4.25); reroll 1–4. Value = (5 + 6)/6 + (4/6) × 4.25 = 11/6 + 17/6 = 28/6 = <b>14/3</b> ≈ 4.67.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Options have value', blocks: [
          `<p>The right to do something later is worth the improvement it brings: value with the option minus value without it. Interviewers often ask "what would you pay for the reroll?"</p>
           <p>Options are worth something because you exercise them only when it helps. The reroll replaces your bad rolls (1, 2, 3) with an average of 3.5 and leaves the good ones alone, so it can only add value. An American option works the same way: the holder exercises early only when the immediate payoff beats the value of waiting, and that choice is worth paying for.</p>`,
          { q: 'What is the option of one reroll worth?', a: 0.75, hint: 'Compare with no reroll.', sol: 'With the reroll the game is worth 4.25; without it, 3.5. The option is worth the difference: 4.25 − 3.5 = <b>$0.75</b>. Check directly: you use it half the time (on 1, 2, 3, averaging 2) and gain 3.5 − 2 = 1.5 each time: ½ × 1.5 = 0.75 ✓.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Costly, unlimited tries', blocks: [
          `<p>If you may reroll forever but each reroll costs c, there's no last decision to start from. But the future always looks the same, so the game's value V satisfies a stationary threshold rule: stop on x when x ≥ V − c. Try thresholds "stop on the top k faces" and pick the best.</p>
           <p>With the top k faces, you roll 6/k times on average, paying c for all but the first, and your final face averages (13 − k)/2.</p>`,
          { q: 'A die pays its face; you may reroll as often as you like for $1 each time. What is the game worth?', a: 4, hint: 'Value of "stop on the top k faces" = (13 − k)/2 − (6/k − 1). Try k = 3 and k = 4.', sol: 'Try each threshold. Top k faces: you roll 6/k times on average and pay for 6/k − 1 rerolls; your final face averages (13 − k)/2. k = 2: 5.5 − 2 = 3.5. k = 3: 5 − 1 = 4. k = 4: 4.5 − 0.5 = 4. k = 5: 4 − 0.2 = 3.8. Best: <b>$4</b>, stopping on 4 or more (a 3 is a coin-flip: keeping it or paying $1 for a game worth $4 both give 3). Check the threshold rule: stop when x ≥ V − c = 3 ✓.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'Why is "keep rolling until you beat your best so far" a bad rule in the costly die game?', opts: [['Past rolls are sunk'], ['It uses too many rolls of a fair die', 'The number of rolls isn\'t the problem by itself.'], ['A higher roll is less likely after a high one', 'Rolls are independent.'], ['It never stops', 'It does stop, at the first six at the latest.']], sol: 'The decision depends only on the state now: this roll against the value of going on. What you\'ve already paid or seen doesn\'t change the future. Letting sunk costs into the decision is a classic trading error too.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'The secretary problem', blocks: [
          `<p>You interview n candidates in random order and must accept or reject each on the spot. You win only if you pick the very best. A good rule: reject the first fraction x, then take the first candidate who beats everyone so far.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 1], y: [0, 0.45], xl: 'fraction of candidates skipped', yl: 'P(pick the best)', xt: [0, [0.368, '1/e'], 1], yt: [0, 0.2, [0.368, '0.37']], fns: [{ f: (x) => (x <= 0 ? 0 : -x * Math.log(x)) }], pts: [{ x: 1 / Math.E, y: 1 / Math.E, tone: 'ok' }], alt: 'The curve minus x log x, peaking at x equals 1 over e with value 1 over e.', cap: 'For large n the success rate is −x ln x, which peaks at x = 1/e, with success rate 1/e. You met this function in the toolkit chapter.' },
          { proof: 'You win if the best is at position t > xn, and the best of those before t sits in the skipped part: chance xn/t. Averaging over t gives ∫ₓ¹ (x/t) dt = −x ln x.' },
          `<p>Why the rule works, in words: if the best candidate is at position t, you pick them exactly when no earlier candidate after the skipped block looked like a "best so far". That happens when the best of the first t − 1 is in the skipped block, which by symmetry has probability (skipped)/(t − 1). Skip too few and you jump at an early "best so far"; skip too many and the true best was probably in the skipped block. 37% balances the two, and it needs no knowledge of how good candidates are, only their ranks.</p>`,
          { q: 'With 100 candidates, about how many should you see and reject before choosing?', a: 37, tol: { abs: 1.5 }, hint: 'n/e.', sol: '100/e ≈ <b>37</b>.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'When the state changes: the red-and-black game', blocks: [
          `<p>Sometimes stopping changes the odds of what's left. A shuffled deck of r red and b black cards is dealt one at a time. Before any card you may stop. Each red card dealt pays you $1 and each black costs $1. When should you stop?</p>
           <p>The state is (red left, black left), and V(r, b) = max(0, expected value of drawing): drawing gives a red with probability r/(r + b), worth 1 + V(r − 1, b), or a black, worth −1 + V(r, b − 1). The boundaries: V(r, 0) = r (take every remaining red) and V(0, b) = 0 (stop now). Fill the table from small decks upwards.</p>
           <p>The surprising part: even with equal numbers, the game is worth something. You can stop as soon as you're ahead, but you're never forced to stop when behind until the deck is spent, at which point you're exactly even.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'A deck has 2 red and 2 black cards. Each red dealt pays $1 and each black costs $1, and you may stop before any card. Playing well, what is the game worth?', a: 2 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'V(1, 1) = ½. Build V(2, 1), V(1, 2), then V(2, 2).', sol: 'V(1, 1) = max(0, ½(1 + 0) + ½(−1 + 1)) = ½. V(2, 1) = ⅔(1 + ½) + ⅓(−1 + 2) = 4/3. V(1, 2) = max(0, ⅓(1 + 0) + ⅔(−1 + ½)) = 0. V(2, 2) = ½(1 + 0) + ½(−1 + 4/3) = <b>2/3</b>. Stop as soon as you\'re ahead; after a black first, keep going.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Explore: more rerolls', blocks: [
          `<p>With more rerolls, the same backward reasoning gives a higher threshold and a higher value.</p>`,
          { widget: 'reroll' },
          { worked: 'continuous version', q: 'You draw from a uniform on [0, 1], and may redraw once. What is the game worth?', steps: [
            ['What is a redraw worth?', '½, its expected value.'],
            ['So what is the threshold?', 'Keep anything above ½.'],
            ['Value: split by the first draw.', 'With probability ½ you keep, averaging ¾. With probability ½ you redraw, for ½.'],
            'V = ½ × ¾ + ½ × ½ = <b>5/8</b>.',
          ] },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Solve the last decision first; its value sets the earlier thresholds. V<sub>n</sub> = E[max(X, V<sub>n−1</sub>)].', 'More chances, higher threshold; each extra chance is worth less.', 'Stop when what you have beats what continuing is worth.', 'Option value = value with it − value without it.', 'Sunk costs don\'t enter the decision.', 'When stopping changes the odds (cards without replacement), the state is what\'s left: build the value table from small cases.', 'Can\'t go back: skip about 37%, then take the next best-so-far.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why must you solve these problems from the end rather than the start?', a: 'Whether to stop now depends on what continuing is worth, and that depends on what you\'ll do later. Only the last decision can be valued on its own, so you start there and work back, using each value as the threshold for the decision before it.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'With three rerolls of a die, what is the game worth?', a: 89 / 18, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'Continuing is worth 14/3 ≈ 4.67, so keep 5 and 6.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> backward induction, using the two-reroll value as the threshold.</p><ol><li>After the first roll, continuing is worth V₂ = 14/3 ≈ 4.67. Keep only 5 and 6.</li><li>V₃ = (5 + 6)/6 + (4/6) × 14/3 = 11/6 + 56/18 = 33/18 + 56/18 = 89/18 ≈ <b>4.94</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Pattern:</b> 3.5 → 4.25 → 4.67 → 4.94. The gains shrink (0.75, 0.42, 0.28) as the value creeps towards 6.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'You roll two dice and take the total. You may reroll both once. What is the game worth?', a: 287 / 36, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'A reroll is worth 7, so keep 8 or more.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> a reroll is worth the average total, 7. Keep anything above 7 (indifferent at 7).</p><ol><li>Totals 8 to 12 occur 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 ways out of 36. Their contribution: (8×5 + 9×4 + 10×3 + 11×2 + 12×1)/36 = (40 + 36 + 30 + 22 + 12)/36 = 140/36.</li><li>The other 21 outcomes (totals 2–7) are replaced by 7: (21/36) × 7 = 147/36.</li><li>Value = 287/36 ≈ <b>7.97</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Option value:</b> 7.97 − 7 ≈ 0.97, more than a single die\'s reroll (0.75), because two dice have more spread to exploit.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A die pays its face, and rerolls cost $0.50 each, as many as you like. What is the game worth?', a: 4.5, hint: 'Use (13 − k)/2 − c(6/k − 1) for each k.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> compare threshold rules "stop on the top k faces".</p><ol><li>k = 1 (wait for a 6): 6 − 0.5 × 5 = 3.5.</li><li>k = 2 (5 or 6): 5.5 − 0.5 × 2 = 4.5.</li><li>k = 3 (4, 5 or 6): 5 − 0.5 × 1 = 4.5.</li><li>k = 4: 4.5 − 0.5 × 0.5 = 4.25.</li><li>Best: <b>$4.50</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> stop when x ≥ V − c = 4.5 − 0.5 = 4. A 4 is the indifferent roll, which is why k = 2 and k = 3 tie.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'What is the value of the second reroll, on top of the first?', a: 14 / 3 - 4.25, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '14/3 − 4.25.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> option value = value with it − value without it.</p><ol><li>Two rerolls: 14/3 ≈ 4.667. One reroll: 4.25.</li><li>Difference: 14/3 − 17/4 = 56/12 − 51/12 = 5/12 ≈ <b>$0.42</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Why less than the first ($0.75):</b> the second reroll only helps when you roll a 4 (you\'d keep it with one reroll, but now replace it), or when a low first roll is followed by a poor reroll. It improves an already good position.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'You draw from a uniform on [0, 1], with two redraws allowed. What is the game worth?', a: 0.375 * 0.8125 + 0.625 * 0.625, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'With one redraw left, the game is worth 5/8, so that\'s your threshold.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> V₂ = E[max(U, V₁)] with V₁ = 5/8.</p><ol><li>Keep the first draw if it beats 5/8: probability 3/8, average (5/8 + 1)/2 = 13/16.</li><li>Otherwise (5/8 of the time) take the one-redraw game, worth 5/8.</li><li>V₂ = (3/8)(13/16) + (5/8)(5/8) = 39/128 + 50/128 = 89/128 ≈ <b>0.695</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Formula:</b> E[max(U, v)] = (1 + v²)/2, so 1/2 → 5/8 → 89/128 → …</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'With 20 candidates in the secretary problem, how many should you skip?', a: 20 / Math.E, tol: { abs: 1 }, hint: 'n/e.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> skip about n/e, then take the first candidate better than everyone so far.</p><ol><li>20/e ≈ 7.4, so skip about <b>7</b>.</li></ol><p>For small n the exact best skip can differ by one from n/e (for 20 it is 7, giving a success rate of about 38%). The success rate stays close to 1/e ≈ 37% however large n gets.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'For the uniform game, show that the values obey v<sub>n+1</sub> = (1 + v<sub>n</sub>²)/2. What is the limit, and why does it make sense?', sol: '<p><b>Derivation:</b> with threshold v = v<sub>n</sub>, you keep a draw above v (probability 1 − v, average (1 + v)/2) or take v.</p><ol><li>v<sub>n+1</sub> = (1 − v)(1 + v)/2 + v × v = (1 − v²)/2 + v² = (1 + v²)/2.</li><li>The limit solves v = (1 + v²)/2, i.e. (v − 1)² = 0, so v = 1.</li></ol><p><b>Why:</b> with endless free tries you can wait for a draw as close to 1 as you like. The approach is slow (0.5, 0.625, 0.695, 0.742, …), roughly 1 − 2/n, because each extra try helps less. With any cost per try, the limit would stop below 1.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Ask a friend to write 20 different numbers on slips, any size. Draw them one at a time, and try to stop on the largest, using the 37% rule. Play 10 times. How often do you win?', sol: '<p>You should win about 3 or 4 times in 10 (the rate is about 38% for 20 slips), far better than the 1 in 20 from guessing.</p><p>The rule needs no knowledge of the range of numbers, which is what makes it surprising: it uses only whether each slip beats the ones before it.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Reroll game', '#/iq/js-reroll'], ['Paid rerolls', '#/iq/js-d100-pay'], ['Secretary problem', '#/iq/cl-secretary'], ['Hat game', '#/iq/wc-hat']],
    },

    continuous: {
      title: 'Continuous probability and geometry', minutes: 45,
      why: 'Random times, random points, broken sticks: with continuous variables, probabilities become lengths and areas, and a good picture often replaces an integral.',
      goals: ['Work with densities and cumulative distributions, and find the density of a transformed variable', 'Turn uniform-random questions into areas', 'Know the order statistics of uniforms', 'Compute expectations by integrating, by the tail integral or by symmetry', 'Solve classic geometric probability: Buffon\'s needle'],
      prereq: ['toolkit', 'distributions', 'symmetry'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Densities and CDFs', blocks: [
          `<p>A continuous variable has a <b>density</b> f(x): the probability of landing in a small interval dx is about f(x) dx, and P(a &lt; X &lt; b) = ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx. Any single point has probability zero. The <b>CDF</b> F(x) = P(X ≤ x) adds up the density from the left, so f = F′. For a uniform on [0, 1], f = 1 and F(x) = x.</p>
           <p>One neat tool: for a non-negative X, E[X] = ∫₀<sup>∞</sup> P(X &gt; t) dt.</p>
           <p>A density is not a probability: it can exceed 1 (a uniform on [0, ½] has density 2). Think of it as probability per unit length. Only its area over an interval is a probability.</p>
           <p><b>The CDF method</b> finds the distribution of anything built from simpler variables: work out P(Y ≤ y) by translating "Y ≤ y" into a statement about the variables you know, then differentiate if you need the density. Example: U uniform on [0, 1] and Y = U². P(Y ≤ y) = P(U ≤ √y) = √y, so Y has density 1/(2√y): most of its mass is near 0, because squaring shrinks small numbers. The same method gives the maximum and minimum below.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'U is uniform on [0, 1]. What is E[√U]?', a: 2 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Average √x over [0, 1]: ∫₀¹ x<sup>½</sup> dx.', sol: '∫₀¹ x<sup>½</sup> dx = [⅔x<sup>3/2</sup>]₀¹ = <b>2/3</b>. It is less than √(E[U]) = √0.5 ≈ 0.707, as it must be: √ is concave, so E[√U] ≤ √E[U] (Jensen).' },
          { worked: 'the tail integral', q: 'What is the expected minimum of two independent uniforms on [0, 1]?', steps: [
            ['P(min > t)?', 'Both must exceed t: (1 − t)².'],
            ['Tail integral?', 'E[min] = ∫₀¹ (1 − t)² dt = [−(1 − t)³/3]₀¹ = ⅓.'],
            'So E[min] = <b>⅓</b>, matching the equal-gaps answer 1/(n + 1) below, and E[max] = 1 − ⅓ = ⅔ by reflection (x ↔ 1 − x).',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Probability as area', blocks: [
          `<p>Two independent uniforms on [0, 1] are a random point in the unit square, so a probability is just the area of a region. Draw it. The meeting problem, the broken stick and many "random time" questions become geometry.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 1], y: [0, 1], w: 270, h: 270, xl: 'your arrival', yl: 'their arrival', xt: [0, 1], yt: [0, 1], polys: [{ pts: [[0, 0], [1 / 6, 0], [1, 5 / 6], [1, 1], [5 / 6, 1], [0, 1 / 6]], tone: 'ok-soft' }], fns: [{ f: (x) => x + 1 / 6, tone: 'ok', to: 5 / 6 }, { f: (x) => x - 1 / 6, tone: 'ok', from: 1 / 6 }], labels: [{ x: 0.5, y: 0.47, t: 'meet', a: 'middle' }, { x: 0.22, y: 0.76, t: 'miss', a: 'middle' }, { x: 0.78, y: 0.2, t: 'miss', a: 'middle' }], alt: 'A unit square with a diagonal band where two arrival times differ by at most ten minutes.', cap: 'They meet in the band |x − y| ≤ 1/6. The two corner triangles fit together into a square of side 5/6.' },
          { widget: 'area' },
          { q: 'X and Y are independent uniforms on [0, 1]. What is P(X + Y < 1.5)?', a: 0.875, hint: 'Only a corner triangle has X + Y ≥ 1.5.', sol: 'Draw the line x + y = 1.5. The region above it is the corner triangle with corners (0.5, 1), (1, 1) and (1, 0.5): legs ½, area ½ × ½ × ½ = ⅛. So P = 1 − ⅛ = <b>0.875</b>.' },
          { q: 'Two people arrive at random times within an hour and each waits 10 minutes. What is the probability they meet?', a: 11 / 36, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'They miss in two corner triangles with legs 5/6.', sol: 'Measure time in hours: they meet when |x − y| ≤ 1/6. The misses are two corner triangles with legs 5/6, each of area ½(5/6)², together (5/6)² = 25/36. So P(meet) = 1 − 25/36 = <b>11/36</b> ≈ 0.31.' },
          `<p>Two habits make these problems routine: <b>draw the square</b> and shade the event, and <b>prefer complements</b> when the event is a big region with small corners cut off. Areas of triangles and rectangles cover most interview questions; only curved boundaries (like xy < ½) need an integral.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'Order statistics', blocks: [
          `<p>Drop n uniform points on [0, 1]. By the equal-gaps argument, they split the interval into n + 1 pieces of equal expected length 1/(n + 1). So E[min] = 1/(n + 1) and E[max] = n/(n + 1).</p>
           <p>For distributions, use this trick: the maximum is below x exactly when <i>all</i> n points are, so P(max ≤ x) = xⁿ.</p>
           <p>Likewise P(min > x) = (1 − x)ⁿ. And in general the k-th smallest of n uniforms has mean k/(n + 1): the k gaps below it each average 1/(n + 1). The k-th smallest has a Beta(k, n + 1 − k) distribution, which you'll meet again in Bayesian estimation.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 1], y: [0, 3.2], xl: 'x', xt: [0, 0.5, 1], yt: [0, 1, 2, 3], fns: [{ f: () => 1, tone: 'muted' }, { f: (x) => 2 * x, tone: 'ok' }, { f: (x) => 3 * x * x, tone: 'acc' }], labels: [{ x: 0.05, y: 1.12, t: 'n = 1' }, { x: 0.58, y: 1.5, t: 'n = 2', tone: 'ok' }, { x: 0.62, y: 2.5, t: 'n = 3', tone: 'acc' }], alt: 'Densities of the maximum of 1, 2 and 3 uniforms, pushing towards 1.', cap: 'Density of the maximum: n xⁿ⁻¹. More points push it towards 1.' },
          { q: 'What is the expected maximum of 3 independent uniforms on [0, 1]?', a: 0.75, hint: 'n/(n + 1).', sol: 'Three points make four gaps of average length ¼, and the maximum sits after three of them: 3/4 = <b>0.75</b>. By integration: the density is 3x², so E = ∫₀¹ 3x³ dx = ¾ ✓.' },
          { q: 'A stick is broken at two uniformly random points. What is the expected length of the shortest piece?', a: 1 / 9, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Find P(shortest > t): every piece must exceed t, which leaves a triangle of side 1 − 3t. Then E = ∫ P(shortest > t) dt.', sol: 'Every piece exceeds t exactly when, after setting aside t for each piece, the remaining length 1 − 3t is split freely: P(shortest > t) = (1 − 3t)² for t < ⅓. Tail integral: ∫₀<sup>⅓</sup> (1 − 3t)² dt = [−(1 − 3t)³/9]₀<sup>⅓</sup> = 1/9 ≈ <b>0.111</b>. (The longest averages 11/18 and the middle one 5/18; the three add to 1.)' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Integrating when you have to', blocks: [
          `<p>E[g(X)] = ∫ g(x) f(x) dx. For a uniform on [0, 1], f = 1, so it's just the average of g over the interval. Look for symmetry first: E[|X − ½|] is the average distance from the middle.</p>`,
          { q: 'X is uniform on [0, 1]. What is E[|X − ½|]?', a: 0.25, hint: '|X − ½| is uniform on [0, ½].', sol: 'Fold the interval at ½: X and 1 − X give the same distance, and |X − ½| is uniform on [0, ½]. Its mean is the midpoint: <b>¼</b>. By integration: 2∫<sub>½</sub>¹ (x − ½) dx = 2 × ⅛ = ¼ ✓.' },
          { worked: 'broken stick', q: 'Break a stick at two uniform points. What is the chance the three pieces form a triangle?', steps: [
            ['What must be true of the pieces for a triangle?', 'No piece can be ½ or longer: each side must be shorter than the sum of the other two.'],
            ['Let the cuts be X &lt; Y. Which conditions does that give?', 'X &lt; ½, Y − X &lt; ½ and Y &gt; ½.'],
            ['Draw it in the triangle X &lt; Y, which has area ½.', 'The allowed region is a small triangle with corners (0, ½), (½, ½) and (½, 1), with area ⅛.'],
            'So P = ⅛ ÷ ½ = <b>¼</b>.',
          ] },
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 1], y: [0, 1], w: 250, h: 250, xl: 'first cut X', yl: 'second cut Y', xt: [0, 0.5, 1], yt: [0, 0.5, 1], polys: [{ pts: [[0, 0], [1, 1], [0, 1]], tone: 'acc-soft' }, { pts: [[0, 0.5], [0.5, 0.5], [0.5, 1]], tone: 'ok' }], alt: 'The upper triangle of the unit square, with a smaller triangle a quarter of its area shaded.', cap: 'The dark triangle is a quarter of the area where X &lt; Y.' },
          `<p>You may recognise the answer: the symmetry chapter got ¼ in three lines by noting that at most one piece can be too long. Two methods agreeing is a good habit to build.</p>`,
          { worked: 'Buffon\'s needle', q: 'A needle of length 1 is dropped on a floor of parallel lines 1 apart. What is the chance it crosses a line?', steps: [
            ['What two random quantities describe the drop?', 'The distance d from the needle\'s centre to the nearest line (uniform on [0, ½]) and its angle θ to the lines (uniform on [0, π/2] by symmetry).'],
            ['When does it cross?', 'When half the needle\'s reach across, ½ sin θ, is more than d.'],
            ['Probability = area of {d < ½ sin θ} over the area of the box ½ × π/2.', '∫₀<sup>π/2</sup> ½ sin θ dθ = ½.'],
            'P = ½ ÷ (π/4) = <b>2/π</b> ≈ 0.637. Dropping needles estimates π, which is how this 18th-century puzzle became the first Monte Carlo method.',
          ] },
          { id: 'x2', q: 'Same floor (lines 1 apart), but the needle has length ½. What is the chance it crosses a line? (Give a decimal.)', a: 1 / Math.PI, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'For a needle of length l ≤ the gap d, P = 2l/(πd).', sol: 'The reach across is now ¼ sin θ, so the area halves: P = 2 × ½/(π × 1) = 1/π ≈ <b>0.318</b>. The chance is proportional to the needle\'s length, which is a clue to an elegant proof: the expected number of crossings is linear in length, for any shape of wire.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'X and Y are independent uniforms on [0, 1]. Which is the most useful first step for P(XY &lt; ½)?', opts: [['Sketch y = 1/(2x) on the square'], ['Average X and Y', 'That\'s a different quantity.'], ['Use the normal approximation', 'There\'s an exact answer from an area.'], ['Multiply P(X &lt; ½) by P(Y &lt; ½)', 'XY can be small without both being small.']], sol: 'The region under y = 1/(2x) in the square has area ½ + ∫<sub>½</sub>¹ 1/(2x) dx = ½ + ½ ln 2 ≈ 0.85.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Probabilities are areas under a density; F′ = f. A density can exceed 1.', 'To find the distribution of a function of variables, compute its CDF first.', 'E[X] = ∫ P(X > t) dt for X ≥ 0.', 'Two uniforms = a point in the square; probabilities are areas.', 'n uniform points: equal expected gaps of 1/(n + 1).', 'E[max of n] = n/(n + 1); E[min] = 1/(n + 1).', 'P(max ≤ x) = P(all ≤ x); P(min > x) = P(all > x); the k-th smallest averages k/(n + 1).', 'Use symmetry before integrating, and check one method with another.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why is P(max of n uniforms ≤ x) = xⁿ? And how would you use a similar trick for the minimum?', a: 'The largest is below x exactly when every point is, and each point is independently below x with probability x. For the minimum, flip it: the smallest is above x exactly when every point is, so P(min &gt; x) = (1 − x)ⁿ.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'X and Y are independent uniforms on [0, 1]. What is P(X + Y &lt; ½)?', a: 0.125, hint: 'A corner triangle.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> the point (X, Y) is uniform in the unit square, so the answer is an area.</p><ol><li>x + y &lt; ½ is the triangle with corners (0, 0), (½, 0) and (0, ½).</li><li>Area = ½ × ½ × ½ = <b>⅛</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check by symmetry:</b> X + Y and 2 − (X + Y) have the same distribution (replace X by 1 − X and Y by 1 − Y), so P(X + Y &lt; ½) = P(X + Y > 1.5) = ⅛, matching the chapter\'s corner triangle.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'What is the probability that the larger of two uniforms is below ½?', a: 0.25, hint: 'xⁿ.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> the larger is below ½ exactly when both are.</p><ol><li>Each is below ½ with probability ½, independently.</li><li>(½)² = <b>¼</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Picture:</b> the bottom-left quarter of the unit square.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'What is the expected minimum of 4 independent uniforms?', a: 0.2, hint: '1/(n + 1).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> equal gaps. Four points split [0, 1] into five gaps of equal expected length.</p><ol><li>Each gap averages 1/5.</li><li>The minimum is the first gap: <b>⅕</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check with the tail integral:</b> P(min > t) = (1 − t)⁴, and ∫₀¹ (1 − t)⁴ dt = ⅕ ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'X and Y are independent uniforms on [0, 1]. What is P(|X − Y| &lt; ½)?', a: 0.75, hint: 'Two corner triangles with legs ½.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> complement. |X − Y| ≥ ½ in two corner triangles of the square.</p><ol><li>Each triangle has legs ½, so area ⅛; together ¼.</li><li>P(|X − Y| &lt; ½) = 1 − ¼ = <b>¾</b>.</li></ol><p>It\'s the meeting problem with a 30-minute wait: 1 − (1 − ½)² = ¾.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'What is E[|X − Y|] for two independent uniforms?', a: 1 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Two points split [0, 1] into three equal expected gaps.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> equal gaps. The two points cut [0, 1] into three pieces of equal expected length ⅓.</p><ol><li>|X − Y| is the middle piece: <b>⅓</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check by integration:</b> E|X − Y| = ∫∫ |x − y| dx dy = 2∫₀¹∫₀<sup>x</sup> (x − y) dy dx = 2∫₀¹ x²/2 dx = ⅓ ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'A point (X, Y) is uniform in the unit square. What is P(X² + Y² &lt; 1)?', a: Math.PI / 4, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'A quarter of a circle of radius 1.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> area. X² + Y² &lt; 1 is the quarter of the unit disc in the square.</p><ol><li>Area = π × 1²/4 = π/4 ≈ <b>0.785</b>.</li></ol><p>This is the basis of the Monte Carlo estimate of π in the lab: 4 × (fraction of points inside).</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'What is E[max(X, Y)²] for two independent uniforms?', a: 0.5, hint: 'The max has density 2x, so integrate x² · 2x.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> find the density of M = max(X, Y), then integrate.</p><ol><li>P(M ≤ x) = x², so the density is 2x.</li><li>E[M²] = ∫₀¹ x² × 2x dx = ∫₀¹ 2x³ dx = 2/4 = <b>½</b>.</li></ol><p><b>A slicker route:</b> max² + min² = X² + Y², so E[max²] + E[min²] = 2/3. And E[min²] = ∫₀¹ x² × 2(1 − x) dx = 1/6, giving E[max²] = 2/3 − 1/6 = ½ ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Three points are chosen at random on a circle. Show that the chance they all lie in some semicircle is ¾.', sol: '<p><b>Proof:</b> split by which point is "first" going clockwise around the semicircle that contains them.</p><ol><li>Fix point A. The three points lie in a semicircle starting at A and going clockwise exactly when the other two both land in that half: probability ½ × ½ = ¼.</li><li>These events for A, B and C can\'t happen together (only one point can be the clockwise-first one of a set that fits in a semicircle), and one of them must happen if the points fit in some semicircle.</li><li>So P = 3 × ¼ = <b>¾</b>.</li></ol><p>For n points the same argument gives n/2<sup>n−1</sup>.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Estimate π by Monte Carlo. Open Python and run: <code>import random; print(4*sum(random.random()**2+random.random()**2&lt;1 for _ in range(10000))/10000)</code>. How does the error change when you use 100 times as many points?', sol: '<p>Each point is inside with probability p = π/4, so the estimate 4 × (fraction inside) has standard error 4√(p(1 − p)/n) ≈ 1.64/√n: about 0.016 at 10,000 points and 0.0016 at a million.</p><p>100 times more data gives 10 times less error: the √n rule again. Monte Carlo is simple and general, but slow to get precise.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Broken stick', '#/iq/wc-stick-triangle'], ['Meeting problem', '#/iq/sq-meeting'], ['Semicircle', '#/iq/cl-semicircle'], ['Distance to an edge', '#/iq/hrt-edge-distance']],
    },
  });
})();
